helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0203.xhp	0	help	par_id3147338	2			0	en-GB	The Tools bar contains frequently used functions.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0203.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151242	3			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/smath/02/03010000.xhp\" name=\"Formula Cursor\"\>Formula Cursor\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	$[officename] Math Features				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155064	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"main0503\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/main0503.xhp\" name=\"$[officename] Math Features\"\>$[officename] Math Features\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	par_id3156386	2			0	en-GB	This section contains an overview of some of the important functions and capabilities that $[officename] Math offers.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	par_id3148486	3			0	en-GB	$[officename] Math provides numerous operators, functions and formatting assistants to help you create formulae. These are all listed in a selection window, in which you can click the required element with the mouse to insert the object into your work. There is an exhaustive \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091500.xhp\" name=\"reference\"\>reference\</link\> list and numerous \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090900.xhp\" name=\"samples\"\>samples\</link\> contained in the Help.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150207	10			0	en-GB	Creating a Formula				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	par_id3145386	12			0	en-GB	As with charts and images, formulae are created as objects within a document. Inserting a formula into a document automatically starts $[officename] Math. You can create, edit and format the formula using a large selection of predefined symbols and functions.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153916	15			0	en-GB	Typing a Formula Directly				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213	13			0	en-GB	If you are familiar with the $[officename] Math language, you can also type a formula directly into the document. For example, type this formula into a text document: "a sup 2 + b sup 2 = c sup 2". Select this text and choose \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\>. The text will be converted into a formatted formula.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	par_id3146325	14			0	en-GB	Formulae cannot be calculated in $[officename] Math because it is a formula editor (for writing and showing formulae) and not a calculation program. Use spreadsheets to calculate formulae, or for simple calculations use the text document calculation function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145829	16			0	en-GB	Creating a Formula in the Commands Window				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	par_id3153001	4			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_COMMAND_WIN_EDIT\"\>Use the $[officename] Math Commands window to enter and edit formulae. As you make entries in the Commands window, you will see the results in the document.\</ahelp\> To maintain an overview when creating long and complicated formulae, use the Formula Cursor on the Tools bar. When this function is activated, the cursor location within the Commands window is also shown in the text window.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150014	17			0	en-GB	Individual Symbols				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	par_id3145246	6			0	en-GB	You can create your own symbols and import characters from other fonts. You can add new symbols to the basic catalogue of $[officename] Math symbols, or create your own special catalogues. Numerous special characters are also available.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148392	18			0	en-GB	Formulae in Context				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\main0503.xhp	0	help	par_id3148774	8			0	en-GB	To make working with formulae easier, use the context menus, which can be called up with a right mouse click. This applies especially to the Command window. This context menu contains all the commands that are found in the Formula Elements window, and also operators, and so on, which can be inserted into your formula by mouse-click without having to key them into the Command window.				20130618 17:22:18
