helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3153956	61			0	en-GB	Use this feature if you are working with values that differ sharply from each other. You can use logarithmic scaling to make the grid lines of the axis equidistant but have values that may increase or decrease.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id9941404				0	en-GB	Reverse direction				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id5581835				0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Defines where the lower and where the higher values are displayed at the axis. The unmarked state is the mathematical direction.\</ahelp\> That means for Cartesian coordinate systems that the x-axis shows the lower values on the left and the y-axis shows the lower values at the bottom. For polar coordinate systems the mathematical angle axis direction is counter-clockwise and the radial axis is from inner to outer.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id922204				0	en-GB	Type				20130618 17:22:18
