helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Manually Aligning Formula Parts				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156384				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>aligning; characters in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula parts; manually aligning\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156384	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"align\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/align.xhp\" name=\"Manually Aligning Formula Parts\"\>Manually Aligning Formula Parts\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657	2			0	en-GB	How do you align characters in $[officename] Math quickly and easily?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3150249	3			0	en-GB	To accomplish this, you must define empty groups and text strings. They do not require any space, but carry information that helps in the alignment process.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153912	4			0	en-GB	To create empty groups, enter curly brackets \<emph\>{}\</emph\> in the Commands window. In the following example, the goal is to achieve a line break so that the plus signs are beneath one another, even though one fewer character is entered in the upper line:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246	7			0	en-GB	a+a+a+{} newline {}{}{}{}{}a+a+a+a				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3143229	5			0	en-GB	Empty text strings are a simple way to ensure that text strings and formulae are left-aligned. They are defined using double inverted commas "" . Ensure you do not use any typographic inverted commas. Example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153809	6			0	en-GB	"A further example." newline a+b newline ""c-d				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Changing Default Attributes				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145792				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>attributes; changing in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>font attributes;changing defaults\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;changing default attributes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defaults;changing default formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing;default formatting\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145792	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"attributes\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/attributes.xhp\" name=\"Changing Default Attributes\"\>Changing Default Attributes\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154484	2			0	en-GB	Can default formats in $[officename] Math be modified?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3148870	3			0	en-GB	Some parts of formulae are always formatted bold or italic by default.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3150210	7			0	en-GB	You can remove these attributes using "nbold" and "nitalic". Example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3143231	6			0	en-GB	a + b				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810	5			0	en-GB	nitalic a + bold b.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872	4			0	en-GB	In the second formula, the a is not italic. The b is bold. You cannot change the plus sign by this method.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Merging Formula Parts in Brackets				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152596				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>brackets; merging formula parts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula parts; merging\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>fractions in formulae\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>merging;formula parts\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152596	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"brackets\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/brackets.xhp\" name=\"Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\"\>Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154511	2			0	en-GB	Inserting fractions into formulae				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3146971	4			0	en-GB	In the case of a fraction whose numerator and denominator consist of a product, a sum, and so on, the values that belong together must be bracketed together.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3149021	3			0	en-GB	Use the following syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3083280	7			0	en-GB	{a + c} over 2 = m				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3154703	6			0	en-GB	or				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3150297	5			0	en-GB	m = {a + c} over 2				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Entering Comments				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155961				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>comments; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;comments in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155961	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"comment\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/comment.xhp\" name=\"Entering Comments\"\>Entering Comments\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657	2			0	en-GB	How does one attach comments that don't appear in the document to a formula?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_id3149499	3			0	en-GB	A comment begins with a double percent sign \<emph\>%%\</emph\>, and extends to the next line-end character (Enter key). Everything that lies in between is ignored and is not printed out. If there are percent signs in the text, they are treated as part of the text.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	en-GB	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D3				0	en-GB	a^2+b^2=c^2 %% Pythagorean theorem.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149018				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>accessibility; $[officename] Math shortcuts\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149018	12			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"keyboard\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/keyboard.xhp\" name=\"Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\"\>Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150298	1			0	en-GB	You can control $[officename] Math without a mouse.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150343	2			0	en-GB	Inserting a Formula Directly				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659	3			0	en-GB	If you want to insert a formula into a text document, and you already know the correct writing, you can proceed as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153818	4			0	en-GB	Write the formula into your text				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153915	5			0	en-GB	Select the formula				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213	6			0	en-GB	Choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154767	7			0	en-GB	Inserting a Formula using a Window				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875	8			0	en-GB	If you want to use the $[officename] Math interface to edit a formula, choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\> without any text selected.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391	9			0	en-GB	The cursor waits in the Commands window and you can type the formula.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150537	13			0	en-GB	You can compose formulae using the Formula Elements window. Open it with the menu \<emph\>View - Formula Elements\</emph\> if it is not already open.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3155625	10			0	en-GB	If the Formula Elements window is open, use F6 to switch from the Commands window to the Formula Elements window and back.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154554	11			0	en-GB	Formula Elements window				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Working with Limits				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	bm_id8404492				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>limits;in sums/integrals\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>integral limits\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id1892147				0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"limits\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/limits.xhp\"\>Working with Limits\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id9881893				0	en-GB	How can I define the limits in a Sum or Integral formula?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6504409				0	en-GB	You want to insert a summation formula like "summation of s^k from k = 0 to n" at the cursor in a Writer text document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8811304				0	en-GB	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1276589				0	en-GB	You will see the Math input window and the Formula Elements window. If you don't see the Formula Elements window, you can enable it in the View menu.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3283791				0	en-GB	In the upper part of the Formula Elements window, click the \<emph\>Operators\</emph\> icon.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9734794				0	en-GB	In the lower part of the Formula Elements window, click the \<emph\>Sum\</emph\> icon.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9641712				0	en-GB	To enable lower and upper limits, click additionally the \<emph\>Upper and Lower Limits\</emph\> icon.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3304239				0	en-GB	In the input window, the first placeholder or marker is selected, and you can start to enter the lower limit:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8679158				0	en-GB	k = 0				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8471327				0	en-GB	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the upper limit:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4731894				0	en-GB	n				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id759300				0	en-GB	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the summand:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id651875				0	en-GB	s^k				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6756187				0	en-GB	Now the formula is complete. Click within your text document outside the formula to leave the formula editor.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9406414				0	en-GB	In the same way, you can enter an Integral formula with limits. When you click an icon from the Formula Elements window, the assigned text command is inserted in the input window. If you know the text commands, you can enter the commands directly in the input window.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id5866267				0	en-GB	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1965697				0	en-GB	Click in the input window and enter the following line:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9004486				0	en-GB	int from{a} to{b} f(x)`dx				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4651020				0	en-GB	A small gap exists between f(x) and dx, which you can also enter using the Formula Elements window: click the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> icon, then the \<emph\>Small Gap\</emph\> icon.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3877071				0	en-GB	If you don't like the font of the letters f and x, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Fonts\</item\> and select other fonts. Click the \<emph\>Default\</emph\> button to use the new fonts as default from now on.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3021332				0	en-GB	If you need the formula within a line of text, the limits increase the line height. You can choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Text Mode\</item\> to place the limits besides the Sum or Integral symbol, which reduces the line height.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id260322				0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090909.xhp\"\>Example of Integral and Sum ranges\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
