helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	bm_id1295205				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>line breaks; in formulae\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulae;line breaks\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>wrapping text;in formulae\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146970	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"newline\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/newline.xhp\" name=\"Entering Line Breaks\"\>Entering Line Breaks\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147339	2			0	en-GB	How to write formulae in $[officename] Math over two lines (with manual line break):				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	par_id3154702	3			0	en-GB	Create a line break by using the "newline" command. Everything coming after the line break is placed on the next line.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Inserting Brackets				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153415				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>brackets; inserting in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;brackets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>distances between brackets\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153415	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"parentheses\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/parentheses.xhp\" name=\"Inserting Brackets\"\>Inserting Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150751	2			0	en-GB	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Math, can brackets be shown separately so that the distance between them is freely definable?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3083281	3			0	en-GB	You can set individual brackets using "left" and "right", but the distance between the brackets will not be fixed, as they adapt to the argument. Nevertheless, there is a way to display brackets so that the distance between them is fixed. To accomplish this, place a "\\" (backslash) before the normal brackets. These brackets now behave like any other symbol and the alignment is the same as with other symbols:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3155960	6			0	en-GB	left lbrace x right none				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3148489	5			0	en-GB	size *2 langle x rangle				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3150344	4			0	en-GB	size *2 \\langle x \\rangle				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Entering Text				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155962				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>text strings; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>direct text; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;text in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id5676442				0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"text\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/text.xhp\" name=\"Entering Text\"\>Entering Text\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id8509170				0	en-GB	How to enter direct text strings that do not get interpreted?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id337229				0	en-GB	Some text strings get interpreted as operators automatically. Sometimes this is not what you want. If you want to write W\<emph\>*\</emph\> (a letter with a superscripted asterisk), the asterisk will be interpreted as a multiplication operator. Enclose the direct text within double quotes or add spaceholders.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	en-GB	Examples				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id521866				0	en-GB	An imported MathType formula contains the following string				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9413739				0	en-GB	W rSup { size 8{*} }				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id755943				0	en-GB	If you have set up Math to convert imported MathType formulae (in \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Load/Save - Microsoft Office), you will see the formula with a placeholder instead of the asterisk.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id5988952				0	en-GB	Change {*} to {} * {} as in the following formula:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id3304544				0	en-GB	W rSup { size 8{} * {} }				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id4941557				0	en-GB	You can also use W^"*" to enter the character as direct text.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9961851				0	en-GB	Some formulae start with an = sign. Use "=" to enter that character as direct text.				20130618 17:22:18
