helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Brackets and Grouping				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147341				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>brackets and grouping in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>grouping and brackets in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147341	1			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091100.xhp\" name=\"Brackets and Grouping\"\>Brackets and Grouping\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150342	2			0	en-GB	Note: the quotation marks in the examples are used to emphasise text and do not belong to the content of the formulae and commands.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146962	3			0	en-GB	When typing example formulae into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window, note that spaces are often required for correct structure.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149054	4			0	en-GB	Braces "{}" are used to group expressions together to form one new expression. For example, "sqrt {x * y}" is the square root of the entire product x*y, while "sqrt x * y" is the square root of x multiplied by y. Braces do not require an extra space.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151392	5			0	en-GB	Set brackets were previously inserted in the Formula Elements window or directly in the Commands window as "left lbrace <?> right rbrace". Now, a left and a right set bracket can also be inserted using "lbrace" and "rbrace", without or without wildcards.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147403	6			0	en-GB	There are a total of eight (8) different types of brackets available. The "ceil" and "floor" brackets are often used for rounding up or down the argument to the next integer: "lceil -3.7 rceil = -3" or "lfloor -3.7 rfloor = -4".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146320	63			0	en-GB	Operator brackets, also known as Bra-kets (angle brackets with a vertical line in between), are common in Physics notation: "langle a mline b rangle" or "langle a mline b mline c over d mline e rangle." The height and positioning of the vertical lines always corresponds exactly to the enclosing brackets.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157870	7			0	en-GB	All brackets may only be used in pairs. The brackets have some common characteristics:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155761	8			0	en-GB	All types of brackets have the same grouping function as described for "{}"\<emph\>-\</emph\> brackets.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145590	9			0	en-GB	All types of brackets, including those that are visible, permit empty group definition. The enclosed expression may therefore be empty.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154562	10			0	en-GB	Brackets do not adjust their size to the enclosed expression. For example, if you want "( a over b )" with a bracket size adjusted to a and b you must insert "left" and "right". Entering "left(a over b right)" produces appropriate sizing. If, however, the brackets themselves are part of the expression whose size is changed, they are included the size change: "size 3(a over b)" and "size 12(a over b)". The sizing of the bracket-to-bracket expression ratio does not change in any way.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153002	11			0	en-GB	Since "left" and "right" ensure unique assignment of the brackets, every single bracket can be used as an argument for these two commands, even placing right brackets on the left side, or left brackets on the right. Instead of a bracket you can use the "none" qualifier, which means that there is no bracket shown and that there is no space reserved for a bracket. Using this, you can create the following expressions:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150014	12			0	en-GB	left lbrace x right none				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149877	13			0	en-GB	left [ x right )				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145241	14			0	en-GB	left ] x right [				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156060	15			0	en-GB	left rangle x right lfloor				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150935	16			0	en-GB	The same rules apply to "left" and "right" as to the other brackets: they also work as group builders and may enclose empty expressions.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149030	17			0	en-GB	The combination of mismatched brackets, single brackets and repositioned left and right brackets occurs often in mathematical formulae. The following is a formula that will create an error when typed:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155989	19			0	en-GB	[2, 3) - right open interval				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147169	23			0	en-GB	Using "left" and "right" makes the above expression valid in $[officename] Math: "left [2, 3 right )". However, the brackets do not have any fixed size because they adjust to the argument. Setting a single bracket is a bit cumbersome. Therefore, there you can display single brackets with a fixed size by placing a "\\" (backslash) in front of normal brackets. These brackets then act like any other symbol and no longer have the special functionality of brackets; that is they do not work as group builders and their orientation corresponds to that of other symbols. See "size *2 \\langle x \\rangle" and "size *2 langle x rangle".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153720	24			0	en-GB	The complete overview is as follows				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149715	25			0	en-GB	\\{ or \\lbrace, \\} or \\rbrace				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150756	26			0	en-GB	\\(, \\)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145207	27			0	en-GB	\\[, \\]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153153	28			0	en-GB	\\langle, \\rangle				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150263	29			0	en-GB	\\lceil, \\rceil				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147252	30			0	en-GB	\\lfloor, \\rfloor				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154690	31			0	en-GB	\\lline, \\rline				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145414	32			0	en-GB	\\ldline, \\rdline				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147514	33			0	en-GB	In this way, intervals like the one above can be built in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> without any problems: \\[2", "3\\) or "\\]2", "3\\[ (Attention: These quotation marks are part of the entry.)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153532	34			0	en-GB	Please note that the quotation marks must be entered and can be obtained with \<emph\>Shift+2\</emph\> and not with typographical quotation marks. Generally, punctuation marks (like the comma in this case) are set as text. Although it is also possible to type "\\[2,~3\\)" the above option is preferable. In the previous example, "fixed size" always describes a bracket size dependent on the font size used.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153674	35			0	en-GB	Nesting groups within each other is relatively problem-free. In the formula hat "{a + b}" the "hat" is displayed simply over the centre of "{a + b}". Also, "colour red lceil a rceil" and "grave hat langle x * y rangle" work as expected. The result of the latter can be compared to "grave {hat langle x * y rangle}." These attributes do not compete, but rather can be combined.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147526	36			0	en-GB	This differs slightly for competing or mutually influencing attributes. This is often the case with font attributes. For example, which colour does the b have in "colour yellow colour red (a + colour green b)", or which size does it have in "size *4 (a + size /2 b)"? Given a base size of 12, does it have the size 48, 6 or even 24 (which could be seen as a combination)? The following are basic resolution rules, which will be followed consistently in the future. In general, the rules apply to all group operations. This only has a visible effect on the font attributes, like "bold", "ital", "phantom", "size", "colour" and "font":				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152952	37			0	en-GB	Group operations in sequence are treated as if every single operation is enclosed by braces { }. They are nested, and in every level there can be no more than one operation. Here is an example of a formula with many group operations: "size 12 colour red font sans size -5 (a + size 8 b)" like "{size 12{colour red{font sans{size -5 (a + {size 8 b})}}}}".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158441	38			0	en-GB	This example formula is then interpreted from left to right. The operations only affect its corresponding group (or expression). Operations further to the right "replace" or "combine themselves with" their predecessors.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150994	39			0	en-GB	A group operation does not have any effect on higher-level operations but rather affects only lower-level groups and expressions, including their brackets and super-/subscripts. For example, "a + size *2 (b * size -8 c_1)^2"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154196	40			0	en-GB	"colour ..." and "font ..." as well as "size n" (n is a decimal) replace any preceding operations of the same type				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154136	41			0	en-GB	for "size +n", "size -n", "size *n", and "size /n" the effects of the operations are combined,				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146934	42			0	en-GB	"size *2 size -5 a" would be double the starting size minus 5				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149297	43			0	en-GB	"font sans ( a + font serif b)"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155174	44			0	en-GB	"size *2 ( a + size /2 b )"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154906	50			0	en-GB	To change the size of a formula, use "size +" or -,*,/. Do not use "size n". These can easily be used in any context. This enables you to copy to other areas by using Copy and Paste, and the result remains the same. Furthermore, such expressions survive a change of base size in the menu better than when using "size n". If you use only "size *" and "size /" (for example, "size *1.24 a or size /0.86 a") the proportions remain intact.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147587	51			0	en-GB	Examples (with a base size of 12 and 50% for indices):				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148734	52			0	en-GB	Exactly identical proportions with "size 18 a_n" and "size *1.5 a_n".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152766	53			0	en-GB	This differs in different contexts: "x^{size 18 a_n}" and "x^{size *1.5 a_n}"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157986	54			0	en-GB	Examples with size +n for a comparison. They look identical:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158001	55			0	en-GB	a_{size 8 n}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332	56			0	en-GB	a_{size +2 n}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155143	57			0	en-GB	a_{size *1.333 n}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147129	58			0	en-GB	The following examples, however, do not look identical:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147073	59			0	en-GB	x^{a_{size 8 n}}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147086	60			0	en-GB	x^{a_{size +2 n}}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154386	61			0	en-GB	x^{a_{size *1.333 n}}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153354	62			0	en-GB	Note that all n here have different sizes. The size 1.333 results from 8/6, the desired size divided by the default index size 6. (Index size 50% with a base size of 12)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Indices and Exponents				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150746				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>indices and exponents in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponents and indices in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150746	1			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"Indices and Exponents\"\>Indices and Exponents\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153730	2			0	en-GB	Here, you will find basic information about indices and exponents in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>. You can try the examples described here to help you understand the details discussed. (The quotation marks in this text are for emphasis purposes only and are not part of the examples.)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149884	3			0	en-GB	The index and exponent for a character are displayed one on top of the other, left-justified to the base character. For example, type \<emph\>a_2^3\</emph\> or \<emph\>a^3_2\</emph\>. This can be in any order. Instead of \<emph\>'_'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'^'\</emph\>, you can use \<emph\>'sub'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'sup'\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148387	4			0	en-GB	However, it is no longer possible to use the following patterns				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149029	5			0	en-GB	a_2_3				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155985	6			0	en-GB	a^2^3				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153923	7			0	en-GB	a_2^3_4				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153724	8			0	en-GB	Each sub-/superscript position of a base character can only be used once. You must use brackets to indicate the desired result. The following examples illustrate this				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185	9			0	en-GB	a_{2_3}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272	10			0	en-GB	a^{2^3}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156316	11			0	en-GB	a_2^{3_4}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145207	12			0	en-GB	a_{2^3}^{4_5}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151173	13			0	en-GB	Unlike other formula editors where "\<emph\>_\</emph\>" and " \<emph\>^\</emph\> " only refer to the next character ("a_24" refers only to the "2"), $[officename] Math refers to the entire number(s)/name(s)/text. If you want to put superscripts and subscripts in sequence, the expression can be written as follows: a_2{}^3 or a^3{}_2				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154260	15			0	en-GB	To write tensors, \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> provides several options. In addition to the notation "R_i{}^{jk}{}_l", common in other applications, additional notations can be used, namely "R_i{}^jk{}_l" and "{{R_i}^jk}_l."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147516	16			0	en-GB	Super- and subscripts to the left of the base character can also be right-justified. To do this, the new commands "lsub" and "lsup" are used. Both commands have the same effect as "sub" and "sup", except that they are left of the base character. See also "a lsub 2 lsup 3."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154276	17			0	en-GB	The rules governing unambiguity and the necessity of using brackets remain the same. In principle, this can be achieved with \<emph\>{}_2^3 a\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152961	18			0	en-GB	The commands "sub" and "sup" are also available as "rsub" and "rsup".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3158437	20			0	en-GB	Using the "csub" and "csup" commands, you can write super- and subscripts directly above or below a character. An example is "a csub y csup x". Combinations of indices and exponents together are also possible: "abc_1^2 lsub 3 lsup 4 csub 55555 csup 66666."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154570	21			0	en-GB	Super- and subscripts can be attached to most unary and binary operators. Two examples: "a div_2 b a<csub n b +_2 h" and "a toward csub f b x toward csup f y."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155904	19			0	en-GB	Be sure to also enter all spaces between characters when entering these examples into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Attributes				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148839				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>attributes; additional information\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148839	1			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091300.xhp\" name=\"Attributes\"\>Attributes\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150051	2			0	en-GB	Additional information about attributes in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> is found here.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146966	3			0	en-GB	The \<emph\>acute\</emph\>, \<emph\>bar\</emph\>, \<emph\>breve\</emph\>, \<emph\>check\</emph\>, \<emph\>circle\</emph\>, \<emph\>dot\</emph\>, \<emph\>ddot\</emph\>, \<emph\>dddot\</emph\>, \<emph\>grave\</emph\>, \<emph\>hat\</emph\>, \<emph\>tilde\</emph\> and \<emph\>vec\</emph\> attributes always have a fixed size and do not become wider (longer) if they are above a long symbol. By default, the attributes are centred.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154557	4			0	en-GB	The only attributes which grow with the length of the symbol are \<emph\>overline\</emph\>, \<emph\>underline\</emph\> and \<emph\>overstrike\</emph\>. (Also, \<emph\>overstrike\</emph\> places a horizontal line through the character and not above it.)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151180	6			0	en-GB	For some text strings, it is possible that a line inserted with \<emph\>underline\</emph\> is too close to the character. In this case, an empty group can solve the problem: \<emph\>underline Q sub {}\</emph\> instead of \<emph\>underline Q\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Scaling				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153923				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>scaling; in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153923	1			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091400.xhp\" name=\"Scaling\"\>Scaling\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147173	2			0	en-GB	More detailed information about scaling in \<emph\>\<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Math\</emph\> as well as some examples can be found here. (The quotation marks in this text are for emphasis purposes only and are not part of the examples.)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3156316	3			0	en-GB	The factorial is not scaled (example: "fact stack{a#b}" and "fact {a over b}") but is oriented using the baseline or centre of the arguments.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3150756	4			0	en-GB	Brackets always have a fixed size as well. This applies to all symbols that can be used as brackets. Compare "(((a)))", "( stack{a#b#c})", "(a over b)."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147570	7			0	en-GB	Brackets preceded by "left" or "right", however, are always adjusted to the argument. See "left(left(left(a right)right)right)", "left(stack{a#b#c}right)", "left(a over b right)."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3145206	11			0	en-GB	Some \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091300.xhp\" name=\"Attributes\"\>Attributes\</link\> have fixed sizes; do not change these if they are placed above a long symbol.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091400.xhp	0	help	par_id3154694	12			0	en-GB	The spaces in the examples are required for the correct structure. You may not delete them when making entries in the Commands window.				20130618 17:22:18
