helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156443	64			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"combine_etc\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/combine_etc.xhp\" name=\"Combining Objects and Constructing Shapes\"\>Combining Objects and Constructing Shapes\</link\> \</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3149020	65			0	en-GB	Combined drawing objects act as grouped objects, except that you cannot enter the group to edit the individual objects. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659	87			0	en-GB	You can only combine 2-D objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150344	32			0	en-GB	To combine 2-D objects:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3166428	66			0	en-GB	Select two or more 2-D objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3145587	67			0	en-GB	Choose \<emph\>Modify - Combine\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3146978	33			0	en-GB	Unlike groups, a combined object takes on the properties of the lowermost object in the stacking order. You can split apart combined objects, but the original object properties are lost.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3155088	34			0	en-GB	When you combine objects, holes appear where the objects overlap.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3156019				0	en-GB	\<image id=\"img_id3157978\" src=\"res/helpimg/kombi1.png\" width=\"8.3646inch\" height=\"2.5inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3157978\"\>Illustration for combining objects\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3153249	35			0	en-GB	In the illustration, the uncombined objects are on the left and the combined objects on the right.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159229	68			0	en-GB	Constructing Shapes				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150049	63			0	en-GB	You can construct shapes by applying the \<link href=\"text/simpress/01/13180000.xhp\" name=\"Shapes\"\>\<emph\>Shapes\</emph\>\</link\> \<emph\>- Merge, Subtract and Intersect\</emph\> commands to two or more drawing objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3147403	88			0	en-GB	Shape commands only work on 2-D objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150539	89			0	en-GB	Constructed shapes take on the properties of the lowermost object in the stacking order.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156402	90			0	en-GB	To construct a shape:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3157874	69			0	en-GB	Select two or more 2-D objects.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150650	70			0	en-GB	Choose \<emph\>Modify - Shapes\</emph\> and one of the following:				20130618 17:22:18
