helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030204.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150792	5			0	en-GB	Return value:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030204.xhp	0	help	par_id3154140	6			0	en-GB	Integer				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030204.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156280	7			0	en-GB	Parameters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030204.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124	8			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that contains the serial time number that is used to calculate the number of seconds.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030204.xhp	0	help	par_id3125864	9			0	en-GB	This function is the opposite of the \<emph\>TimeSerial\</emph\> function. It returns the seconds of a serial time value that is generated by the \<emph\>TimeSerial\</emph\> or \<emph\>TimeValue\</emph\> functions. For example, the expression:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030204.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951	10			0	en-GB	Print Second(TimeSerial(12, 30, 41))				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030204.xhp	0	help	par_id3151117	11			0	en-GB	returns the value 41.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030204.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147426	12			0	en-GB	Example:				20130618 17:22:18
