helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030204.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112	15			0	en-GB	End sub				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	TimeSerial Function [Run-time]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	bm_id3143271				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>TimeSerial function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143271	1			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030205.xhp\" name=\"TimeSerial Function [Run-time]\"\>TimeSerial Function [Run-time]\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3156344	2			0	en-GB	Calculates a serial time value for the specified hour, minute and second parameters that are passed as numeric values. You can then use this value to calculate the difference between times.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146794	4			0	en-GB	Syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3150792	5			0	en-GB	TimeSerial (hour, minute, second)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148797	6			0	en-GB	Return value:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3154908	7			0	en-GB	Date				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154124	8			0	en-GB	Parameters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193	9			0	en-GB	\<emph\>hour:\</emph\> An integer expression that indicates the hour of the time that is used to determine the serial time value. Values in the range 0 to 23 are valid.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252	10			0	en-GB	\<emph\>minute:\</emph\> An integer expression that indicates the minute of the time that is used to determine the serial time value. In general, use values in the range 0 to 59. However, you can also use values that lie outside of this range, where the number of minutes influence the hour value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3161831	11			0	en-GB	\<emph\>second:\</emph\> An integer expression that indicates the second of the time that is used to determine the serial time value. In general, you can use values in the range 0 to 59. However, you can also use values that lie outside of this range, where the number seconds influences the minute value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854	12			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Examples:\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153952	13			0	en-GB	12, -5, 45 corresponds to 11, 55, 45				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3147349	14			0	en-GB	12, 61, 45 corresponds to 13, 2, 45				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3147426	15			0	en-GB	12, 20, -2 corresponds to 12, 19, 58				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153365	16			0	en-GB	12, 20, 63 corresponds to 12, 21, 4				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985	17			0	en-GB	You can use the TimeSerial function to convert any time into a single value that you can use to calculate time differences.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3155308	18			0	en-GB	The TimeSerial function returns the type Variant with VarType 7 (Date). This value is stored internally as a double-precision number in the range 0 to 0.9999999999. As opposed to the DateSerial or DateValue function, where the serial date values are calculated as days relative to a fixed date, you can calculate with values returned by the TimeSerial function, but you cannot evaluate them.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3149482	19			0	en-GB	In the TimeValue function, you can pass a string as a parameter containing the time. For the TimeSerial function, however, you can pass the individual parameters (hour, minute, second) as separate numeric expressions.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154790	20			0	en-GB	Example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3145252	21			0	en-GB	Sub ExampleTimeSerial				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157	22			0	en-GB	Dim dDate As Double, sDate As String				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3156286	23			0	en-GB	dDate = TimeSerial(8,30,15)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3148456	24			0	en-GB	sDate = TimeSerial(8,30,15)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3155600	25			0	en-GB	MsgBox dDate,64,"Time as a number"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153417	26			0	en-GB	MsgBox sDate,64,"Formatted time"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153836	27			0	en-GB	End Sub				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	TimeValue Function [Run-time]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149670				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>TimeValue function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149670	1			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030206.xhp\" name=\"TimeValue Function [Run-time]\"\>TimeValue Function [Run-time]\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	par_id3153361	2			0	en-GB	Calculates a serial time value from the specified hour, minute and second - parameters passed as strings - that represents the time in a single numeric value. This value can be used to calculate the difference between times.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154138	3			0	en-GB	Syntax:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	par_id3156282	4			0	en-GB	TimeValue (Text As String)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153969	5			0	en-GB	Return value:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	par_id3156424	6			0	en-GB	Date				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145172	7			0	en-GB	Parameters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	par_id3145786	8			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Text:\</emph\> Any string expression that contains the time that you want to calculate in the format "HH:MM:SS".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578	9			0	en-GB	Use the TimeValue function to convert any time into a single value, so that you can calculate time differences.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710	10			0	en-GB	This TimeValue function returns the type Variant with VarType 7 (Date) and stores this value internally as a double-precision number in the range 0 to 0.9999999999.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	par_id3151117	11			0	en-GB	As opposed to the DateSerial or the DateValue function, where serial date values result in days relative to a fixed date, you can calculate with the values that are returned by the TimeValue function, but you cannot evaluate them.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	par_id3147426	12			0	en-GB	In the TimeSerial function, you can pass individual parameters (hour, minute, second) as separate numeric expressions. For the TimeValue function, however, you can pass a string as a parameter containing the time.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030206.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145271	13			0	en-GB	Example:				20130618 17:22:18
