helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id8716086				0	en-GB	Meaning				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id3978087				0	en-GB	A black line				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id4065065				0	en-GB	\<image id=\"img_id9379863\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_7.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id9379863\"\>solid line for user-defined border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id6987823				0	en-GB	A black line sets the corresponding line of the selected cells. The line is shown as a dotted line when you choose the 0.05 pt line style. Double lines are shown when you select a double line style.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id1209143				0	en-GB	A grey line				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id6653340				0	en-GB	\<image id=\"img_id6972563\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_gray.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id6972563\"\>grey line for user-defined border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2278817				0	en-GB	A grey line is shown when the corresponding line of the selected cells will not be changed. No line will be set or removed at this position.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5374919				0	en-GB	A white line				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id52491				0	en-GB	\<image id=\"img_id3801080\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_white.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3801080\"\>white line for user-defined border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id372325				0	en-GB	A white line is shown when the corresponding line of the selected cells will be removed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	hd_id7282937				0	en-GB	Examples				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id4230780				0	en-GB	Select a single cell, then choose \<emph\>Format - Cells - Borders\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id1712393				0	en-GB	Click the lower edge to set a very thin line as a lower border. All other lines will be removed from the cell.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5149693				0	en-GB	\<image id=\"img_id9467452\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_6.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id9467452\"\>setting a thin lower border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5759453				0	en-GB	Choose a thicker line style and click the lower edge. This sets a thicker line as a lower border.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id6342051				0	en-GB	\<image id=\"img_id7431562\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_7.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id7431562\"\>setting a thick line as a border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5775322				0	en-GB	Click the second \<emph\>Default\</emph\> icon from the left to set all four borders. Then repeatedly click the lower edge until a white line is shown. This removes the lower border.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2882778				0	en-GB	\<image id=\"img_id8155766.00000001\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_8.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id8155766.00000001\"\>removing lower border\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id8102053				0	en-GB	You can combine several line types and styles. The last image shows how to set thick outer borders (the thick black lines), while any diagonal lines inside the cell will not be touched (grey lines).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2102420				0	en-GB	\<image id=\"img_id5380718\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_9.png\" width=\"1.2602in\" height=\"1.1252in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id5380718\"\>advanced example for cell borders\</alt\>\</image\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Calculating With Dates and Times				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146120				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>dates; in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>times; in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells;date and time formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>current date and time values\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146120	11			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"calc_date\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_date.xhp\" name=\"Calculating With Dates and Times\"\>Calculating With Dates and Times\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3154320	12			0	en-GB	In $[officename] Calc, you can perform calculations with current date and time values. As an example, to find out exactly how old you are in seconds or hours, follow the following steps:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150750	13			0	en-GB	In a spreadsheet, enter your birthday in cell A1.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3145642	14			0	en-GB	Enter the following formula in cell A3: \<item type=\"literal\"\>=NOW()-A1\</item\>            				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149020	52			0	en-GB	After pressing the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key you will see the result in date format. Since the result should show the difference between two dates as a number of days, you must format cell A3 as a number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335	53			0	en-GB	Place the cursor in cell A3, right-click to open a context menu and choose \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343	54			0	en-GB	The \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format Cells\</item\> dialogue box will appear. On the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Numbers\</item\> tab, the "Number" category will appear already highlighted. The format is set to "General", which causes the result of a calculation containing date entries to be displayed as a date. To display the result as a number, set the number format to "-1,234" and close the dialogue box using the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\> button.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147001	15			0	en-GB	The number of days between today's date and the specified date is displayed in cell A3.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304	16			0	en-GB	Experiment with some additional formulae: in A4 enter =A3*24 to calculate the hours, in A5 enter =A4*60 for the minutes, and in A6 enter =A5*60 for seconds. Press the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key after each formula.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149207	17			0	en-GB	The time since your date of birth will be calculated and displayed in the various units. The values are calculated as of the exact moment when you entered the last formula and pressed the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key. This value is not automatically updated, although "Now" continuously changes. In the \<emph\>Tools\</emph\> menu, the menu item \<emph\>Cell Content - AutoCalculate\</emph\> is normally active; however, automatic calculation does not apply to the function NOW. This ensures that your computer is not solely occupied with updating the sheet.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Automatically Calculating Series				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>series; calculating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; series\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>linear series\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>growth series\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>date series\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>powers of 2 calculations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; filling automatically\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>automatic cell filling\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>AutoFill function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>filling;cells, automatically\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769	6			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"calc_series\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_series.xhp\" name=\"Automatically Calculating Series\"\>Automatically Filling in Data Based on Adjacent Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A8				0	en-GB	You can automatically fill cells with data with the AutoFill command or the Series command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D3				0	en-GB	Using AutoFill				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D7				0	en-GB	AutoFill automatically generates a data series based on a defined pattern.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319	7			0	en-GB	On a sheet, click in a cell, and type a number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CB				0	en-GB	Click in another cell and then click back in the cell where you typed the number.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272	16			0	en-GB	Drag the fill handle in the bottom right corner of the cell across the cells that you want to fill, and release the mouse button.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801	17			0	en-GB	The cells are filled with ascending numbers.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EE				0	en-GB	To quickly create a list of consecutive days, enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>Monday\</item\> in a cell, and drag the fill handle.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id9720145				0	en-GB	Hold down \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>if you do not want to fill the cells with different values.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490	18			0	en-GB	If you select two or more adjacent cells that contain different numbers, and drag, the remaining cells are filled with the arithmetic pattern that is recognised in the numbers. The AutoFill function also recognises customised lists that are defined under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10737				0	en-GB	You can double-click the fill handle to automatically fill all empty columns of the current data block. For example, first enter Jan into A1 and drag the fill handle down to A12 to get the twelve months in the first column. Now enter some values into B1 and C1. Select those two cells, and double-click the fill handle. This fills automatically the data block B1:C12.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10713				0	en-GB	Using a Defined Series				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749	9			0	en-GB	Select the cell range in the sheet that you want to fill.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754	19			0	en-GB	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit - Fill - Series\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10716				0	en-GB	Select the parameters for the series. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10731				0	en-GB	If you select a \<emph\>linear\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>added\</emph\> to each consecutive number in the series to create the next value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN1073C				0	en-GB	If you select a \<emph\>growth\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>multiplied\</emph\> by each consecutive number to create the next value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10747				0	en-GB	If you select a \<emph\>date\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is added to the time unit that you specify.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3159173	20			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060400.xhp\" name=\"Sort lists\"\>Sort lists\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Calculating Time Differences				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;time differences\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>time differences\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769	53			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"calc_timevalues\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_timevalues.xhp\" name=\"Calculating Time Differences\"\>Calculating Time Differences\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3149263	54			0	en-GB	If you want to calculate time differences, for example, the time between 23:30 and 01:10 in the same night, use the following formula:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3159153	55			0	en-GB	=(B2<A2)+B2-A2				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3152598	56			0	en-GB	The later time is B2 and the earlier time is A2. The result of the example is 01:40 or 1 hour and 40 minutes.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3145271	57			0	en-GB	In the formula, an entire 24-hour day has a value of 1 and one hour has a value of 1/24. The logical value in parentheses (round brackets) is 0 or 1, corresponding to 0 or 24 hours. The result returned by the formula is automatically issued in time format due to the sequence of the operands.				20130618 17:22:18
