helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154346				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>text in cells; multi-line\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; text breaks\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>breaks in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>multi-line text in cells\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154346	42			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"text_wrap\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/text_wrap.xhp\" name=\"Writing Multi-line Text\"\>Writing Multi-line Text\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280	41			0	en-GB	Pressing the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Enter keys inserts a manual line break. This shortcut only works directly in the cell, not in the input line.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3153142	43			0	en-GB	If you want the text to automatically break at the right border of the cell, proceed as follows:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951	44			0	en-GB	Select all the cells where you want the text to break at the right border.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575	45			0	en-GB	In \<emph\>Format - Cells - Alignment\</emph\>, mark the \<emph\>Wrap text automatically\</emph\> option and click OK.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799	46			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05020000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cell\"\>Format - Cell\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	User-Defined Functions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155411				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>functions; user-defined\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>user-defined functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic IDE for user-defined functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>IDE; Basic IDE\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>programming;functions\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155411	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"userdefined_function\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/userdefined_function.xhp\" name=\"Defining Functions Yourself\"\>User-Defined Functions\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3153969	2			0	en-GB	You can apply user-defined functions in $[officename] Calc in the following ways:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145366	4			0	en-GB	You can define your own functions using the Basic-IDE. This method requires a basic knowledge of programming.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768	3			0	en-GB	You can program functions as \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060111.xhp\" name=\"add-ins\"\>add-ins\</link\>. This method requires an advanced knowledge of programming.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149260	6			0	en-GB	Defining A Function Using %PRODUCTNAME Basic				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148456	7			0	en-GB	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organise Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510	8			0	en-GB	Click the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> button. You will now see the Basic IDE.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327	9			0	en-GB	Enter the function code. In this example, we define a \<item type=\"literal\"\>VOL(a; b; c)\</item\> function that calculates the volume of a rectangular solid with side lengths \<item type=\"literal\"\>a\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>b\</item\> and \<item type=\"literal\"\>c\</item\>:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443	10			0	en-GB	Close the Basic-IDE window.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150043	11			0	en-GB	Your function is automatically saved in the default module and is now available. If you apply the function in a Calc document that is to be used on another computer, you can copy the function to the Calc document as described in the next section.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147340	18			0	en-GB	Copying a Function To a Document				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145232	19			0	en-GB	In stage 2 of "Defining A Function Using %PRODUCTNAME Basic", in the \<emph\>Macro\</emph\> dialogue box you clicked on \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>. As the default, in the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field the \<emph\>My Macros - Standard - Module1\</emph\> module is selected. The \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> library resides locally in your user directory.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3154022	20			0	en-GB	If you want to copy the user-defined function to a Calc document:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304	21			0	en-GB	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organise Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\> .				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150086	22			0	en-GB	In the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field select \<emph\>My Macros - Standard - Module1\</emph\> and click \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3166430	23			0	en-GB	In the Basic-IDE, select the source of your user-defined function and copy it to the clipboard.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_idN1081D				0	en-GB	Close the Basic-IDE.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150517	24			0	en-GB	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organise Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\> .				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145384	25			0	en-GB	In the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field select \<emph\>(Name of the Calc document) - Standard - Module1\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148699	26			0	en-GB	Paste the clipboard contents in the Basic-IDE of the document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153305	12			0	en-GB	Applying a User-Defined Function in $[officename] Calc				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148869	13			0	en-GB	Once you have defined the function \<item type=\"literal\"\>VOL(a; b; c)\</item\> in the Basic-IDE, you can apply it the same way as the built-in functions of $[officename] Calc.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148606	14			0	en-GB	Open a Calc document and enter numbers for the function parameters \<item type=\"literal\"\>a\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>b\</item\>, and \<item type=\"literal\"\>c\</item\> in cells A1, B1, and C1.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3156019	15			0	en-GB	Set the cursor in another cell and enter the following:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3155264	16			0	en-GB	=VOL(A1;B1;C1)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3146776	17			0	en-GB	The function is evaluated and you will see the result in the selected cell.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Validity of Cell Content				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156442				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>values; limiting on input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>limits; specifying value limits on input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>permitted cell content\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data validity\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>validity\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; validity\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>error messages; defining for incorrect input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>actions in case of incorrect input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Help tips; defining text for cell input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>comments;help text for cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; defining input help\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>macros; running when incorrect input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data; validity check\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156442	22			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"validity\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/validity.xhp\" name=\"Validity of Cell Content\"\>Validity of Cell Content\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3156283	2			0	en-GB	For each cell, you can define in advance what type of content is valid for that cell. This allows you to guide users through data entry in $[officename] Calc by restricting cells to receive specific values and ranges.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3145252	3			0	en-GB	The validity rule is activated when a new value is entered. If an invalid value has already been inserted into the cell, or if you insert a value in the cell either with drag-and-drop or by copying and pasting, the validity rule will not take effect.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id5174718				0	en-GB	You can choose \<emph\>Tools - Detective\</emph\> at any time and choose the command \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/06030800.xhp\" name=\"Mark Invalid Data\"\>\<emph\>Mark Invalid Data\</emph\>\</link\> to display which cells contain invalid values.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155603	5			0	en-GB	Using Cell Content Validity				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3155959	6			0	en-GB	Select the cells for which you want to define a new validity rule. You can select multiple cells by clicking on all the cells while holding down the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Ctrl\</item\> key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3148837	8			0	en-GB	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Validity\</item\>. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3156020	9			0	en-GB	On the \<emph\>Criteria\</emph\> tab page, enter the conditions for new values entered into cells. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3159208	10			0	en-GB	In the \<emph\>Allow\</emph\> field, choose from one of the other options listed to place a condition on a cell entry.  "All Values" is selected by default.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3153011	11			0	en-GB	If you choose "Whole Numbers", values such as "12.5" are not allowed, even if they satisfy the other conditions. Choosing "Date" allows date information both in the local date format as well as in the form of a \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030101.xhp\" name=\"serial date\"\>serial date\</link\>. Similarly, the "Time" condition permits time values such as "12:00" or serial time numbers. "Text Length" stipulates that cells are allowed to contain text strings only.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id9224829				0	en-GB	Select "List" to enter a list of valid entries.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3149317	13			0	en-GB	Select the next condition under \<emph\>Data\</emph\>. According to what you choose, additional text fields (\<emph\>Value\</emph\>, \<emph\>Minimum\</emph\> and \<emph\>Maximum\</emph\>) may appear to further refine the condition.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3151389	15			0	en-GB	After you have determined the conditions for cell validity, you can use the other two tab pages to create input help and error/invalid entry message boxes:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3159261	16			0	en-GB	On the \<emph\>Input Help\</emph\> tab page, enter the title and the text of the tip, which will then be displayed if the cell is selected.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3156396	17			0	en-GB	On the \<emph\>Error Alert\</emph\> tab page, select the action to be carried out in the event of an error.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3147416	18			0	en-GB	If you select "Stop" as the action, invalid entries are not accepted, and the previous cell content is retained.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3150033	19			0	en-GB	Select "Warning" or "Information" to display a dialogue box in which the entry can either be cancelled (the cell value will be retained) or accepted (even if the new value violates the validity rule).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3149947	20			0	en-GB	If you select "Macro", then by using the \<emph\>Browse\</emph\> button you can specify a macro to be run in the event of an error.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3149011	35			0	en-GB	To display the error message, select \<emph\>Show error message when invalid values are entered\</emph\>. 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3148586	21			0	en-GB	After changing the action for a cell on the \<emph\>Error Alert\</emph\> tab page and closing the dialogue box with OK, you must first select another cell before the change takes effect.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3154805	30			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12120000.xhp\" name=\"Data - Validity\"\>Data - Validity\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Naming Cells				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147434				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>cells; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>names; defining for cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>values; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>constants definition\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;names for cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulae; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>addressing; by defined names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell names; defining/addressing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>references; by defined names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>allowed cell names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>renaming;cells\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147434	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"value_with_name\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/value_with_name.xhp\" name=\"Naming Cells\"\>Naming Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id4391918				0	en-GB	Allowed names				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id2129581				0	en-GB	Names in Calc can contain letters, numeric characters, and some special characters. Names must start with a letter or an underline character.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id1120029				0	en-GB	Allowed special characters:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3362224				0	en-GB	underline (_) 				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4891506				0	en-GB	dot (.) - allowed within a name, but not as first or last character				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id2816553				0	en-GB	blank ( ) - allowed within a name, but not as first or last character, and not for a cell range				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id328989				0	en-GB	Names must not be the same as cell references. For example, the name A1 is invalid because A1 is a cell reference to the top left cell.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id32898987				0	en-GB	Names must not start with the letter R followed by a number. See the ADDRESS function for more information.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4769737				0	en-GB	Names for cell ranges must not include blanks. Blanks are allowed within names for single cells, sheets and documents.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id1226233				0	en-GB	Naming cells and formulae				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id5489364				0	en-GB	A good way of making the references to cells and cell ranges in formulae legible is to give the ranges names. For example, you can name the range A1:B2 \<emph\>Start\</emph\>. You can then write a formula such as "=SUM(Start)". Even after you insert or delete rows or columns, $[officename] still correctly assigns the ranges identified by name. Range names must not contain any spaces.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id953398				0	en-GB	For example, it is much easier to read a formula for value-added tax if you can write "= Amount * VAT_rate" instead of "= A5 * B12". In this case, you would name cell A5 "Amount" and cell B12 "VAT_rate."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4889675				0	en-GB	Use the \<emph\>Define Names\</emph\> dialogue box to define names for formulae or parts of formulae you need more often. In order to specify range names,				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153954	3			0	en-GB	Select a cell or range of cells, then choose \<emph\>Insert - Names - Define\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Define Names\</emph\> dialogue box will appear.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3156283	4			0	en-GB	Type the name of the selected area in the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> field. Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>. The newly defined name appears in the list below. Click OK to close the dialogue box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id5774101				0	en-GB	You can also name other cell ranges in this dialogue box by entering the name in the field and then selecting the respective cells.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154942	5			0	en-GB	If you type the name in a formula, after the first few characters entered you will see the entire name as a tip.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510	6			0	en-GB	Press the Enter key in order to accept the name from the tip.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749	7			0	en-GB	If more than one name starts with the same characters, you can scroll through all the names using the Tab key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153711	8			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04070100.xhp\" name=\"Insert - Names - Define\"\>Insert - Names - Define\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Inserting External Data in Table (WebQuery)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154346				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>HTML WebQuery\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ranges; inserting in tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>external data; inserting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; inserting external data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>web pages; importing data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>WebQuery filter\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting; external data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data sources; external data\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125863	2			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"webquery\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/webquery.xhp\" name=\"Inserting External Data in Table (WebQuery)\"\>Inserting External Data in Table (WebQuery)\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3155131	3			0	en-GB	With the help of the \<emph\>Web Page Query ($[officename] Calc)\</emph\> import filter, you can insert tables from HTML documents in a Calc spreadsheet.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575	4			0	en-GB	You can use the same method to insert ranges defined by name from a Calc or Microsoft Excel spreadsheet.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664	5			0	en-GB	The following insert methods are available:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146976	6			0	en-GB	Inserting by Dialogue Box				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319	7			0	en-GB	Set the cell cursor at the cell where the new content will be inserted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3145750	8			0	en-GB	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Link to External Data\</emph\>. This opens the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04090000.xhp\"\>External Data\</link\> dialogue box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958	9			0	en-GB	Enter the URL of the HTML document or the name of the spreadsheet. Press Enter when finished. Click the \<emph\>...\</emph\> button to open a file selection dialogue box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400	10			0	en-GB	In the large list box of the dialogue box, select the named ranges or tables you want to insert.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064	11			0	en-GB	You can also specify that the ranges or tables are updated every n seconds.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443	30			0	en-GB	The import filter can create names for cell ranges on the fly. As much formatting as possible is retained, while the filter intentionally does not load any images.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149021	12			0	en-GB	Inserting by Navigator				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3153965	14			0	en-GB	Open two documents: the $[officename] Calc spreadsheet in which the external data is to be inserted (target document) and the document from which the external data derives (source document).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3150205	16			0	en-GB	In the target document open the Navigator.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3152990	18			0	en-GB	In the lower combo box of the Navigator select the source document. The Navigator now shows the range names and database ranges or the tables contained in the source document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3148842	20			0	en-GB	In the Navigator select the \<emph\>Insert as link\</emph\> drag mode \<image id=\"img_id3152985\" src=\"sw/imglst/sc20238.png\" width=\"0.222inch\" height=\"0.222inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3152985\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3157978	22			0	en-GB	Drag the desired external data from the Navigator into the target document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3144768	23			0	en-GB	If you have loaded an HTML document with the \<emph\>Web Page Query\</emph\> filter as the source document, you will find the tables in the Navigator, named continuously from "HTML_table1" onwards, and also two range names that have been created:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3152873	24			0	en-GB	\<item type=\"literal\"\>HTML_all\</item\> - designates the entire document				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149897	25			0	en-GB	\<item type=\"literal\"\>HTML_tables\</item\> - designates all HTML tables in the document				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149126	26			0	en-GB	Editing the external data				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3159228	27			0	en-GB	Open \<emph\>Edit - Links\</emph\>. Here you can edit the link to the external data.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3154650	28			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04090000.xhp\" name=\"External data dialogue box\"\>External data dialogue box\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	19xx/20xx Years				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150439				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>years; 2-digits\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>dates; 19xx/20xx\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150439	18			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"year2000\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/year2000.xhp\" name=\"19xx/20xx Years\"\>19xx/20xx Years\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151116	17			0	en-GB	The year in a date entry is often entered as two digits. Internally, the year is managed by $[officename] as four digits, so that in the calculation of the difference from 01/01/99 to 01/01/01, the result will correctly be two years.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154011	19			0	en-GB	Under \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - $[officename] - General\</emph\> you can define the century that is used when you enter a year with only two digits. The default is 1930 to 2029.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010	20			0	en-GB	This means that if you enter a date of 01/01/30 or higher, it will be converted to the internal date 1930-01-01 or higher. All lower two-digit years are converted to the 20xx century. So, for example, 01/01/20 is converted into 2020-01-01.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\00\00000004.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	To access this function...				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\00\00000004.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155535	1			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"wie\"\>To access this function... \</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\00\00000004.xhp	0	help	par_idN1056E				0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"moreontop\"\>More explanations at the top of this page. \</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\00\00000004.xhp	0	help	par_idN105AF				0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"optional\"\>In the %PRODUCTNAME Calc functions, parameters marked as "optional" can be omitted only when no parameter follows. For example, in a function with four parameters, in which the last two parameters are marked as "optional", you can omit parameter 4 or parameters 3 and 4, but you cannot omit parameter 3 alone. \</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\00\00000004.xhp	0	help	par_id9751884				0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"codes\"\>Codes greater than 127 may depend on your system's character mapping (for example iso-8859-1, iso-8859-2, Windows-1252, Windows-1250) and hence may not be portable.\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
