helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158446				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>MUNIT function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158446	12			0	en-GB	MUNIT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154121	13			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EINHEITSMATRIX\"\>Returns the unitary square array of a certain size.\</ahelp\> The unitary array is a square array where the main diagonal elements equal 1 and all other array elements are equal to 0.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155123	14			0	en-GB	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3156271	15			0	en-GB	MUNIT(Dimensions)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159390	16			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Dimensions\</emph\> refers to the size of the array unit.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10C9B				0	en-GB	You can find a general introduction to Array functions at the top of this page.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156162	17			0	en-GB	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150949	18			0	en-GB	Select a square range within the spreadsheet, for example, from A1 to E5.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151260	19			0	en-GB	Without deselecting the range, select the MUNIT function. Mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box. Enter the desired dimensions for the array unit, in this case \<item type=\"input\"\>5\</item\>, and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150403	20			0	en-GB	You can also enter the \<item type=\"input\"\>=Munit(5)\</item\> formula in the last cell of the selected range (E5), and press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Shift+Command+Enter\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Shift+Ctrl+Enter\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3156143	21			0	en-GB	You now see a unit array with a range of A1:E5.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10FA7				0	en-GB	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159084				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>FREQUENCY function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159084	22			0	en-GB	FREQUENCY				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145777	23			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_HAEUFIGKEIT\"\>Indicates the frequency distribution in a one-column-array.\</ahelp\> The function counts the number of values in the Data array that are within the values given by the Classes array.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153347	24			0	en-GB	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155498	25			0	en-GB	FREQUENCY(Data; Classes)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154352	26			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Data\</emph\> is the array of or reference to the set of values to be counted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148402	27			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Classes\</emph\> is the array of limit values.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D71				0	en-GB	You can find a general introduction to Array functions at the top of this page.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148981	28			0	en-GB	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155904	219			0	en-GB	In the following table, column A lists unsorted measurement values. Column B contains the upper limit that you entered for the classes into which you want to divide the data in column A. According to the limit entered in B1, the FREQUENCY function returns the number of measured values less than or equal to 5. As the limit in B2 is 10, the FREQUENCY function returns the second result as the number of measured values that are greater than 5 and less than or equal to 10. The text that you entered in B6, ">25", is only for reference purposes.				20130618 17:22:18
