helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3144687	132			0	en-GB	Column A contains several X1 values, column B several X2 values and column C the y-values. You have already entered these values in your spreadsheet. You have now set up E2:G6 in the spreadsheet and activated the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>. For the LINEST function to work, you must have marked the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>. Next, select the following values in the spreadsheet (or enter them using the keyboard):				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158020	133			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Data_Y\</emph\> is C2:C8				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158039	134			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Data_X\</emph\> is A2:B8				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158058	135			0	en-GB	\<emph\>LinearType\</emph\> and \<emph\>Stats\</emph\> are both set to 1.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158084	136			0	en-GB	As soon as you click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>, $[officename] Calc will fill the above example with the LINEST values as shown in the example.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158106	137			0	en-GB	The formula in the \<emph\>Formula\</emph\> Bar corresponds to each cell of the LINEST array \<item type=\"input\"\>{=LINEST(C2:C8;A2:B8;1;1)}\</item\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158128	138			0	en-GB	\<emph\>This represents the calculated LINEST values:\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158146				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>slopes, see also regression lines\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>regression lines;LINEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158146	139			0	en-GB	E2 and F2: Slope m of the regression line y = b + m*x for the x1 and x2 values. The values are given in reverse order; that is, the slope for x2 in E2 and the slope for x1 in F2.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158184	140			0	en-GB	G2: Intersection b with the y-axis.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158204				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>standard errors;array functions\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158204	141			0	en-GB	E3 and F3: The standard error of the slope value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145845	142			0	en-GB	G3: The standard error of the intercept				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145859				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>RSQ calculations\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145859	143			0	en-GB	E4: RSQ				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145880	144			0	en-GB	F4: The standard error of the regression calculated for the y-value.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145894	145			0	en-GB	E5: The F value from the variance analysis.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145915	146			0	en-GB	F5: The degrees of freedom from the variance analysis.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145937	147			0	en-GB	E6: The sum of the squared deviation of the estimated y-values from their linear mean.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145952	148			0	en-GB	F6: The sum of the squared deviation of the estimated y-value from the given y-values.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11B04				0	en-GB	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id1596728				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>LOGEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146009	150			0	en-GB	LOGEST				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3146037	151			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_RKP\"\>This function calculates the adjustment of the entered data as an exponential regression curve (y=b*m^x).\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146056	152			0	en-GB	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163123	153			0	en-GB	LOGEST(DataY; DataX; FunctionType; Stats)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163137	154			0	en-GB	\<emph\>DataY\</emph\> is the y-data array.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163155	155			0	en-GB	\<emph\>DataX\</emph\> (optional) is the x-data array.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163174	156			0	en-GB	\<emph\>FunctionType\</emph\> (optional). If FunctionType = 0, functions of the form y = m^x will be calculated. Otherwise, y = b*m^x functions will be calculated.				20130618 17:22:18
