helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149566				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>ISLEAPYEAR function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>leap year determination\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149566	14			0	en-GB	ISLEAPYEAR				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3150297	15			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Determines whether a year is a leap year.\</ahelp\> If yes, the function will return the value 1 (TRUE); if not, it will return 0 (FALSE).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148487	16			0	en-GB	Syntax				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3150205	17			0	en-GB	ISLEAPYEAR("Date")				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3159239	18			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Date\</emph\> specifies whether a given date falls within a leap year. The Date parameter must be a valid date according to the locale settings of %PRODUCTNAME.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149817	19			0	en-GB	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3150786	20			0	en-GB	=ISLEAPYEAR(A1) returns 1, if A1 contains 1968-02-29, the valid date 29th February 1968 in your locale setting.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_idN107E7				0	en-GB	You may also use =ISLEAPYEAR("1968-02-29") or =ISLEAPYEAR("29/02/68").				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_idN107EA				0	en-GB	Never use =ISLEAPYEAR(29/02/68), because this would first evaluate 29 divided by 2 divided by 68, and then calculate the ISLEAPYEAR function from this small number as a serial date number.				20130618 17:22:18
