helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163804	13			0	en-GB	\<switchinline select=\"appl\"\>\<caseinline select=\"IMPRESS\"\>Show notes \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3157909	14			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:CHECKBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_NOTES\"\>Specifies that your notes are also displayed.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156117	15			0	en-GB	Automatic				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149233	16			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_KIOSK\"\>Creates a default HTML presentation as a kiosk export, in which the slides are automatically advanced after a specified amount of time.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155421	17			0	en-GB	As stated in document				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275	18			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CHG_DEFAULT\"\>The slide transition depends on the timing that you set for each slide in the presentation. If you set a manual page transition, the HTML presentation introduces a new page by pressing any key from your keyboard.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156307	19			0	en-GB	Automatic				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153126	20			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CHG_AUTO\"\>The page transition takes place automatically after the specified period of time elapses and does not depend on the presentation's contents\</ahelp\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150503	21			0	en-GB	Slide view time				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151245	22			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:TIMEFIELD:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_DURATION_TMF\"\>Defines the amount of time for each slide display.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154347	23			0	en-GB	Endless				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149655	24			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:CHECKBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_ENDLESS\"\>Automatically restarts the HTML presentation after the last slide has been displayed.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151054	25			0	en-GB	WebCast				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150543	26			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_WEBCAST\"\>In a WebCast export, automatic scripts will be generated with Perl or ASP support.\</ahelp\> This enables the speaker (for example, a speaker in a telephone conference using a slide show on the Internet) to change the slides in the audience's web browsers. You will find more information on \<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01110200.xhp\" name=\"WebCast\"\>WebCast\</link\> later in this section.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154365	27			0	en-GB	Active Server Pages (ASP)				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148922	28			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_ASP\"\>When you select the \<emph\>ASP\</emph\> option, the WebCast export creates ASP pages. Note that the HTML presentation can only be offered by a web server supporting ASP.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149765	29			0	en-GB	Perl				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145174	30			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_PERL\"\>Used by WebCast export to create HTML pages and Perl scripts.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150868	31			0	en-GB	URL for listeners				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149203	32			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:EDIT:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_INDEX\"\>Specifies the URL (absolute or relative) to be entered by the viewer in order to see the presentation.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147228	33			0	en-GB	URL for presentation				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156214	34			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:EDIT:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_URL\"\>Specifies the URL (absolute or relative), where the created HTML presentation on the web server has been saved.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153367	35			0	en-GB	URL for Perl scripts				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159255	36			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:EDIT:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CGI\"\>Specifies the URL (absolute or relative) for the generated Perl scripts.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150486	37			0	en-GB	More Information on WebCast Export				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578	38			0	en-GB	There are two possible options for exporting $[officename] Impress presentations using WebCast technology: Active Server Pages (ASP) and Perl.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153364	71			0	en-GB	In either case, the WebCast needs an HTTP server offering either Perl or ASP as scripting. Therefore, the exporting option depends on the HTTP server used.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151112	39			0	en-GB	WebCast in ASP				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159197	40			0	en-GB	Exporting				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119	41			0	en-GB	To export to ASP, in a $[officename] Impress document choose \<emph\>File - Export\</emph\>. You then see the \<emph\>Export\</emph\> dialogue box in which you select \<emph\>HTML Document\</emph\> as the file type. Once you have selected a directory and entered a file name, click \<emph\>Save\</emph\> (or \<emph\>Export\</emph\>). For export as ASP, we recommend selecting a "secret" file name for the HTML file (see below for more details). You then see the \<emph\>HTML Export\</emph\> dialogue box. Several files will be written to the directory you have just selected.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149410	42			0	en-GB	The presenter uses the entered file name to change between the slides viewed by the audience. You can save the WebCast files locally or save them directly to an HTTP server. You can later transfer locally saved files to the HTTP server by FTP. Note that WebCast only works if the files are requested over an HTTP server.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3157974	43			0	en-GB	Do not use the same directory for two different HTML exports.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157	44			0	en-GB	Select \<emph\>WebCast\</emph\> as a publishing type on the second page of the HTML Export Wizard.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154790	45			0	en-GB	In the options area for WebCast, select the \<emph\>Active Server Pages (ASP)\</emph\> option. You can now continue defining other settings or start the export by clicking the \<emph\>Create\</emph\> button.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153281	46			0	en-GB	Using ASP WebCast				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149910	47			0	en-GB	You can use WebCast as soon as the exported files can be accessed from an HTTP server.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154503	48			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Example\</emph\>:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152375	49			0	en-GB	Let's assume that you installed the Microsoft Internet Information Server on your computer. You entered the "c:\\Inet\\wwwroot\\presentation" directory as an HTML output directory during the IIS setup. The URL of your computer is assumed as follows: "http://myserver.com".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715	50			0	en-GB	You have saved the files that have been created during the Export process in the c:\\Inet\\wwwroot\\presentation\\ directory. In this directory, the Export creates an HTML file that can be named, for example, as "secret.htm". You entered this name in the Save dialogue box (see above). The presenter can now browse to the HTML Export files by entering the http://myserver.com/presentation/secret.htm URL in any HTTP Browser having JavaScript support. The presenter is now able to modify the page using some form controls.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159110	51			0	en-GB	The audience can now view the slide selected by the presenter through the URL http://myserver.com/presentation/webcast.asp. They cannot move to other slides found at this URL, unless the file names are known. Please ensure that the HTTP server does not show the directory listing.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153732	52			0	en-GB	WebCast over Perl				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152999	53			0	en-GB	Exporting				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146972	54			0	en-GB	To export, in a $[officename] Impress document choose \<emph\>File - Export\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>Export\</emph\> dialogue box, in which you select \<emph\>HTML Document\</emph\> as the file type. After selecting a folder and entering a file name, click \<emph\>Save\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>HTML Export Wizard\</emph\>. This will write some files to the folder you have just selected.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156362	55			0	en-GB	The entered file name will be used by the presenter to switch through the slides. Please select an empty directory.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148432	56			0	en-GB	In the second page of the HTML Export, select \<emph\>WebCast\</emph\> as the publication type.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149018	57			0	en-GB	In the option area for WebCast, select \<emph\>Perl\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154509	58			0	en-GB	In the \<emph\>URL for listeners\</emph\> text box, enter the file name of the HTML document that will be used by the audience. In \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\>, enter the URL of the directory that will be used for the presentation and, in \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\>, enter the URL for the CGI script directory. You can now define further settings on the following pages of the Wizard or start the export process by clicking the \<emph\>Create\</emph\> button.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148996	59			0	en-GB	Using Perl WebCast				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159268	60			0	en-GB	The files that have been created during the export must now be set up in the Perl enabled HTTP server. This cannot be done automatically because of the variety of different HTTP servers having Perl support. The steps to follow will be described next. Please refer to your server manual or ask your network administrator how to apply these steps on your server.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147340	61			0	en-GB	You should first move the files that have been created during the export into the correct directory on the HTTP server.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155936	62			0	en-GB	Move all files having the htm, jpg and gif extensions into the directory on your HTTP server that has been referred to in the text box \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151014	63			0	en-GB	All files having the pl and txt extensions have to be moved into the directory on your HTTP server that has been referred to in the \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\> text box. This directory must be configured in a way that the Perl scripts contained there can also be run by an HTTP request.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151250	64			0	en-GB	On UNIX systems grant the files with the pl extension the rights to be executable by the HTTP server. Normally, this is done with the chmod command. The rights of the currpic.txt file must be set to be writable by the HTTP server.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153534	65			0	en-GB	Now you should be able to use WebCast.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156711	66			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Example\</emph\>:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147313	67			0	en-GB	In this example, you have a Linux computer with an HTTP server. The URL of your HTTP server is http://myserver.com and the output directory of your HTML documents is the //user/local/http/ directory. Your Perl scripts are contained in the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory. Enter secret.htm as an export file name and presentation.htm as \<emph\>URL for listeners\</emph\>. In the \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\> text box enter http://myserver.com/presentation/ and for the \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\> enter http://myserver.com/cgi-bin/.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145792	68			0	en-GB	Now, copy all *.htm, *.jpg and *.gif files from the directories that were specified during the export into the //user/local/http/presentation/ directory on your HTTP Server and copy all files with the *.pl and *.txt extensions into the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153920	69			0	en-GB	Login on your server as root and switch to the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory. You can define the corresponding rights using the chmod command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148479	70			0	en-GB	Once you have finished installing the Perl files, the presenter will be able to give the presentation. The listeners can view this presentation under the URL http://myserver.com/presentation/presentation.htm.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	HTML Export - Page 3				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153323	27			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01110300.xhp\" name=\"HTML Export - Page 3\"\>HTML Export - Page 3\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147102	28			0	en-GB	Specifies the graphics type and the target screen resolution.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155341	11			0	en-GB	Save graphics as				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3157909	12			0	en-GB	Determines the image format. You can also define the compression value for the export.				20130618 17:22:18
