helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151101	188			0	en-GB	After you close the \<emph\>Find & Replace\</emph\> dialogue box, you can still search using the last search criteria that you entered, by pressing Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+F.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	List of Regular Expressions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146765				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>regular expressions; list of\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>lists;regular expressions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>replacing;tab stops (regular expressions)\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tab stops;regular expressions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>concatenation, see ampersand symbol\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>ampersand symbol, see also operators\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146765	203			0	en-GB	\<variable id=\"02100001\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/01/02100001.xhp\"\>List of Regular Expressions\</link\>\</variable\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3149741	17			0	en-GB	Character				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3155577	18			0	en-GB	Result/Use				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id6600543				0	en-GB	Any character				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id9824518.00000001				0	en-GB	Represents any single character unless otherwise specified.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3152427	19			0	en-GB	.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3149031	20			0	en-GB	Represents any single character except for a line break or paragraph break. For example, the search term "sh.rt" returns both "shirt" and "short".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3154682	21			0	en-GB	^				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3155351	22			0	en-GB	Only finds the search term if the term is at the beginning of a paragraph. Special objects such as empty fields or character-anchored frames, at the beginning of a paragraph are ignored. Example: "^Peter".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3159194	23			0	en-GB	$				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3152542	24			0	en-GB	Only finds the search term if the term appears at the end of a paragraph. Special objects such as empty fields or character-anchored frames at the end of a paragraph are ignored. Example: "Peter$".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3156414	25			0	en-GB	*				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3155555	26			0	en-GB	Finds zero or more of the characters in front of the "*". For example, "Ab*c" finds "Ac", "Abc", "Abbc", "Abbbc", and so on.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3147399	27			0	en-GB	+				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3157958	28			0	en-GB	Finds one or more of the characters in front of the "+". For example, "AX.+4" finds "AXx4", but not "AX4".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3145313	207			0	en-GB	The longest possible string that matches this search pattern in a paragraph is always found. If the paragraph contains the string "AX 4 AX4", the entire passage is highlighted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3143267	199			0	en-GB	?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153684	200			0	en-GB	Finds zero or one of the characters in front of the "?". For example, "Texts?" finds "Text" and "Texts" and "x(ab|c)?y" finds "xy", "xaby", or "xcy".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3166410	158			0	en-GB	\\				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3147209	159			0	en-GB	Search interprets the special character that follows the "\\" as a normal character and not as a regular expression (except for the combinations \\n, \\t, \\>, and \\<). For example, "tree\\." finds "tree.", not "treed" or "trees".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3152945	166			0	en-GB	\\n				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153700	167			0	en-GB	Represents a line break that was inserted with the Shift+Enter key combination. To change a line break into a paragraph break, enter \<emph\>\\n\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> and \<emph\>Replace with\</emph\> boxes, and then perform a search and replace.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id9262672				0	en-GB	\\n in the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> text box stands for a line break that was inserted with the Shift+Enter key combination.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id2366100				0	en-GB	\\n in the \<emph\>Replace with\</emph\> text box stands for a paragraph break that can be entered with the Enter or Return key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153258	29			0	en-GB	\\t				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3157809	30			0	en-GB	Represents a tab. You can also use this expression in the \<emph\>Replace with\</emph\> box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3150670	35			0	en-GB	\\b				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153666	36			0	en-GB	Match a word boundary. For example, "\\bbook" finds "bookmark" but not "handbook" whereas "book\\b" finds "handbook" but not "bookmark". The discrete word "book" is found by both search terms.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3149576	37			0	en-GB	^$				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3151245	38			0	en-GB	Finds an empty paragraph.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3148550	41			0	en-GB	^.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3159413	42			0	en-GB	Finds the first character of a paragraph.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3147282	43			0	en-GB	& or $0				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153961	44			0	en-GB	Adds the string that was found by the search criteria in the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> box to the term in the \<emph\>Replace with\</emph\> box when you make a replacement.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3149650	201			0	en-GB	For example, if you enter "window" in the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> box and "&frame" in the \<emph\>Replace with\</emph\> box, the word "window" is replaced with "windowframe".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3150543	206			0	en-GB	You can also enter an "&" in the \<emph\>Replace with\</emph\> box to modify the \<emph\>Attributes\</emph\> or the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> of the string found by the search criteria.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3145419	172			0	en-GB	[abc123]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3154630	173			0	en-GB	Represents one of the characters that are between the square brackets.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3156293	174			0	en-GB	[a-e]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3149167	175			0	en-GB	Represents any of the characters in the range a to e (inclusive).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id100520090232005				0	en-GB	The characters are ordered by their code numbers.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3155994	176			0	en-GB	[a-eh-x]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3148676	177			0	en-GB	Represents any of the characters in the ranges a to e and h to x (inclusive).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3145318	178			0	en-GB	[^a-s]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153351	179			0	en-GB	Represents any character that is not in the range a to s.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3156543	180			0	en-GB	\\xXXXX				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768	181			0	en-GB	Represents a special character based on its four-digit hexadecimal code (XXXX).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252	208			0	en-GB	The code for the special character depends on the font used. You can view the codes by choosing \<emph\>Insert - Special Character\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951	186			0	en-GB	|				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3154985	187			0	en-GB	Finds the terms that occur before or after the "|". For example, "this|that" finds "this" and "that".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3147376	209			0	en-GB	{2}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3150103	210			0	en-GB	Defines the number of times that the character in front of the opening curly bracket occurs. For example, "tre{2}" finds and selects "tree".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3151289	211			0	en-GB	{1,2}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3147317	212			0	en-GB	Defines the minimum and maximum number of times that the character in front of the opening curly bracket can occur. For example, "tre{1,2}" finds and selects both "tre" and "tree".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id4870754				0	en-GB	{1,}				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id843836				0	en-GB	Defines the minimum number of times that the character in front of the opening curly bracket can occur. For example, "tre{2,}" finds "tree", "treee", and "treeeee".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3148616	213			0	en-GB	( )				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id2701803				0	en-GB	In the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> box:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153573	214			0	en-GB	Defines the characters inside the parentheses (round brackets) as a reference. You can then refer to the first reference in the current expression with "\\1", to the second reference with "\\2", and so on.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3156061	215			0	en-GB	For example, if your text contains the number 13487889 and you search using the regular expression (8)7\\1\\1, "8788" is found.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id2367931				0	en-GB	You can also use () to group terms, for example, "a(bc)?d" finds "ad" or "abcd".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id9200109				0	en-GB	In the \<emph\>Replace with\</emph\> box:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id5766472				0	en-GB	Use $ (dollar) instead of \\ (backslash) to replace references. Use $0 to replace the whole found string.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3154790	226			0	en-GB	[:alpha:]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3147397	227			0	en-GB	Represents an alphabetic character. Use [:alpha:]+ to find one of them.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3152885	216			0	en-GB	[:digit:]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010	217			0	en-GB	Represents a decimal digit. Use [:digit:]+ to find one of them.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153743	224			0	en-GB	[:alnum:]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153281	225			0	en-GB	Represents an alphanumeric character ([:alpha:] and [:digit:]).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3153726	218			0	en-GB	[:space:]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3150961	219			0	en-GB	Represents a space character (but not other whitespace characters).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3150486	220			0	en-GB	[:print:]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3150872	221			0	en-GB	Represents a printable character.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854	222			0	en-GB	[:cntrl:]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3152576	223			0	en-GB	Represents a non-printing character.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958	228			0	en-GB	[:lower:]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3145730	229			0	en-GB	Represents a lower-case character if \<emph\>Match case\</emph\> is selected in \<emph\>Options\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3148455	230			0	en-GB	[:upper:]				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id3150092	231			0	en-GB	Represents an upper-case character if \<emph\>Match case\</emph\> is selected in \<emph\>Options.\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	hd_id5311441				0	en-GB	Examples				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id956834773				0	en-GB	e([:digit:])?    -- finds 'e' followed by zero or one digit. Note that currently all named character classes like [:digit:] must be enclosed in parentheses.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id952368773				0	en-GB	^([:digit:])$    -- finds lines or cells with exactly one digit.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id9568773				0	en-GB	You can combine the search terms to form complex searches.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	hd_id71413				0	en-GB	To find three-digit numbers alone in a paragraph				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id2924283				0	en-GB	^[:digit:]{3}$				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id5781731				0	en-GB	^ means the match must be at the beginning of a paragraph,				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id6942045				0	en-GB	[:digit:] matches any decimal digit,				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id4721823				0	en-GB	{3} means there must be exactly 3 copies of "digit",				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\01\02100001.xhp	0	help	par_id5806756				0	en-GB	$ means the match must end a paragraph.				20130618 17:22:18
