helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Queries				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150445				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>queries;overview (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables in databases; printing queries (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>printing; queries (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>queries; printing (Base)\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150445	1			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02000000.xhp\" name=\"Queries\"\>Queries\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150499	2			0	en-GB	A "query" is a special view of a table. A query can display certain records or only a few selected fields per record and, if required, also sort those records. A query can apply to one table to multiple tables, if they are linked by common data fields.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3147399	12			0	en-GB	Use queries to find records from data tables based on certain criteria. All queries created for a database are listed under the \<emph\>Queries\</emph\> entry. Since this entry contains the database queries, it is also called the "query container".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153750	39			0	en-GB	Printing Queries				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149183	40			0	en-GB	To print a query or table:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156426	41			0	en-GB	Open a text document (or a spreadsheet document if you prefer the specific printing functions of this type of document).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149827	42			0	en-GB	Open the database file and click the Table icon if you want to print a table, or click the Query icon if you want to print a query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149398	43			0	en-GB	Drag the name of the table or query into the open text document or spreadsheet. The dialogue box \<link href=\"text/shared/02/12070000.xhp\" name=\"Insert Database Columns\"\>Insert Database Columns\</link\> opens.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150443	44			0	en-GB	Decide which columns = data fields you want to include. You can also click the \<emph\>AutoFormat\</emph\> button and select a corresponding formatting type. Close the dialogue box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153561	49			0	en-GB	The query or table will be inserted into your document.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150503	50			0	en-GB	Print the document by choosing \<emph\>File - Print\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153146	47			0	en-GB	You can also open the data source view (F4), select the entire database table in the data source view (button in the top left corner of the table), and then drag the selection to a text document or spreadsheet.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148946	46			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/shared/main0212.xhp\" name=\"Sorting and Filtering Data\"\>Sorting and Filtering Data\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149655	28			0	en-GB	Allows you to sort and filter the data in a query table.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153379	14			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"Query Design\"\>Query Design\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151211	18			0	en-GB	With the \<emph\>Query Design\</emph\>, you can create and edit a query or view.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153968	29			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"Query Through Several Tables\"\>Query Through Several Tables\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151043	30			0	en-GB	The query result can contain data from several tables if these are linked to each other by suitable data fields.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159149	31			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"Formulating Query Criteria\"\>Formulating Query Criteria\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154910	32			0	en-GB	You can find out which operators and commands can be used to formulate the filter conditions for a query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156212	33			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"Executing Functions\"\>Executing Functions\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000000.xhp	0	help	par_id3144762	34			0	en-GB	You can perform calculations with the data of a table and store the results as a query result.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Missing Element				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150445				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>queries; missing elements (Base)\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150445	1			0	en-GB	Missing Element				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3150247	2			0	en-GB	If a query in which tables or fields no longer exist is opened, the \<emph\>Missing Element\</emph\> dialogue box will appear. This dialogue box names the missing table or the field which cannot be interpreted and allows you to decide how to continue with the procedure.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145072	3			0	en-GB	How to continue?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149177	4			0	en-GB	There are three options available for answering this question:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147576	5			0	en-GB	Do you really want to open the query in the graphic view?				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3166461	6			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Allows you to open the query in the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"Design View\"\>Design View\</link\> in spite of missing elements.\</ahelp\> This option also allows you to specify if other errors need to be ignored.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153031	7			0	en-GB	The query is opened in the Design View (the graphical interface). Missing tables appear blank and invalid fields appear with their (invalid) names in the list of fields. This lets you work with exactly those fields that caused the error.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149578	8			0	en-GB	Open the query in the SQL View				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3159157	9			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Allows you to open the query design in the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"SQL Mode\"\>SQL Mode\</link\> and to interpret the query as a \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14030000.xhp\" name=\"Native SQL\"\>Native SQL\</link\>.\</ahelp\> You can only quit the native SQL mode when the $[officename] statement is completely interpreted (only possible if the used tables or fields in the query really exist).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150984	10			0	en-GB	Do not open the query				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3156329	11			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Allows you to cancel the procedure and specify that the query should not be opened.\</ahelp\> This option corresponds to the function of the \<emph\>Cancel\</emph\> dialogue box button.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148492	12			0	en-GB	Also ignore similar errors				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154285	13			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>If you selected the first option, but you still want to open the query in the graphics view in spite of missing elements, you can specify whether other errors are ignored.\</ahelp\> Therefore, in the current opening process, no error message will be displayed if the query can not be correctly interpreted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Query Design				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153323				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>views; creating database views (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>queries; creating in design view (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>designing; queries (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>design view; queries/views (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>joining;tables (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables in databases; joining for queries (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>queries; joining tables (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables in databases; relations (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>relations; joining tables (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>queries; deleting table links (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>criteria of query design (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>queries; formulating filter conditions (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>filter conditions;in queries (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters; queries (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>queries; parameter queries (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>SQL; queries (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>native SQL (Base)\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153394	1			0	en-GB	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"Query Design\"\>Query Design\</link\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156411	288			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>The \<emph\>Query Design View\</emph\> allows you to create and edit a database query.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id7024140				0	en-GB	Most databases use queries to filter or to sort database tables to display records on your computer. Views offer the same functionality as queries, but on the server side. If your database is on a server that supports views, you can use views to filter the records on the server to speed up the display time.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159176	276			0	en-GB	Selecting the \<emph\>Create View\</emph\> command from the \<emph\>Tables\</emph\> tab page of a database document, you will see the \<emph\>View Design\</emph\> window that resembles the \<emph\>Query Design\</emph\> window described here.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id8307138				0	en-GB	The Query Design window layout is stored with a created query, but cannot be stored with a created view.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149233	3			0	en-GB	The Design View				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145673	4			0	en-GB	To create a query, click the \<emph\>Queries\</emph\> icon in a database document, then click \<emph\>Create Query in Design View\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150255	5			0	en-GB	The lower pane of the Design View is where you \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"define\"\>define\</link\> the query. To define a query, specify the database \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"field names\"\>field names\</link\> to include and the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"criteria\"\>criteria\</link\> for displaying the fields. To rearrange the columns in the lower pane of the Design View, drag a column header to a new location, or select the column and press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+arrow key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152474	6			0	en-GB	In the top of the query Design View window, the \<link href=\"text/shared/main0214.xhp\" name=\"icons\"\>icons\</link\> of the \<emph\>Query Design\</emph\> Bar and the \<emph\>Design\</emph\> bar are displayed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147559	287			0	en-GB	If you want to test a query, double-click the query name in the database document. The query result is displayed in a table similar to the Data Source View. Note: the table displayed is only temporary.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id8226264				0	en-GB	Keys in Query Design View				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id2341074				0	en-GB	Key				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id4384289				0	en-GB	Function				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id5839106				0	en-GB	F4				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id8554338				0	en-GB	Preview				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id1254921				0	en-GB	F5				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id7636646				0	en-GB	Run Query				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id8579363				0	en-GB	F7				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3227942				0	en-GB	Add Table or Query				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154939	7			0	en-GB	Browse				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148799	8			0	en-GB	When you open the query design for the first time, in order to create a new query, you can click \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14020100.xhp\" name=\"Add Tables\"\>\<emph\>Add Tables\</emph\>\</link\>. You then see a dialogue box in which you must first select the table that will be the basis for the query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144762	10			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_CTL_QRYDGNTAB\"\>Double-click fields to add them to the query. Drag-and-drop to define relations.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157894	141			0	en-GB	While designing a query, you cannot modify the selected tables.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149562	11			0	en-GB	Remove tables				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150685	12			0	en-GB	To remove the table from Design View, click the upper border of the table window and display the context menu. You can use the \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> command to remove the table from the Design View. Another option is to press the Delete key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150012	142			0	en-GB	Move table and modify table size				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146922	143			0	en-GB	You can resize and arrange the tables according to your preferences. To move tables, drag the upper border to the desired position. Enlarge or reduce the size in which the table is displayed by positioning the mouse cursor on a border or on a corner and dragging the table until it is the desired size.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145365	13			0	en-GB	Table Relations				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154145	14			0	en-GB	If there are data relations between a field name in one table and a field name in another table, you can use these relations for your query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577	15			0	en-GB	If, for example, you have a spreadsheet for articles identified by an article number, and a spreadsheet for customers in which you record all articles that a customer orders using the corresponding article numbers, then there is a relationship between the two "article number" data fields. If you now want to create a query that returns all articles that a customer has ordered, you must retrieve data from two spreadsheets. To do this, you must tell $[officename] what the relationship exists between the data in the two spreadsheets.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155302	16			0	en-GB	To do this, click a field name in a table (for example, the field name "Item-Number" from the Customer table), hold down the mouse button and then drag the field name to the field name of the other table ("Item-Number" from the Item table). When you release the mouse button, a line connecting the two fields in the two windows will appear. The corresponding condition that the content of the two field names must be identical is entered in the resulting SQL query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876	137			0	en-GB	The creation of a query that is based on several related sheets is only possible if you use $[officename] as the interface for a relational database.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145646	246			0	en-GB	You cannot access tables from different databases in a query. Queries involving multiple tables can only be created within one database.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153279	224			0	en-GB	Specifying link type				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154791	225			0	en-GB	If you double-click the line connecting two linked fields or call the menu command \<emph\>Insert - New Relation\</emph\>, you can specify the type of link in the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010101.xhp\" name=\"Relations\"\>\<emph\>Relations\</emph\>\</link\> dialogue box.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150094	285			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QUERY_EDIT_JOINCONNECTION\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Edit Join Properties.\</ahelp\>Alternatively, press Tab until the line is selected, then press Shift+F10 to display the context menu and there choose the command \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>. Some databases support only a subset of the possible join types.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155851	145			0	en-GB	Deleting relations				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156178	146			0	en-GB	To delete a relation between two tables, click the connection line and then press the Delete key.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715	284			0	en-GB	Alternatively, delete the respective entries in \<emph\>Fields involved\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Relations\</emph\> dialogue box. Or press Tab until the connecting vector is displayed highlighted, then press Shift+F10 to open the context menu and select \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151208	17			0	en-GB	Define query				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158416	18			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_CTL_QRYDGNCRIT\"\>Select conditions to define the query.\</ahelp\> Each column of the design table accepts a data field for the query. The conditions in one row are linked with a Boolean AND.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154161	19			0	en-GB	Specify field name				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146791	20			0	en-GB	First, select all field names from the tables that you want to add to the query. You can do this either with drag-and-drop or by double-clicking a field name in the table window. With the drag-and-drop method, use the mouse to drag a field name from the table window into the lower area of the query design. As you do this, you can decide which column you want to add the field to. Select a field name by double-clicking. It will then be added to the next free column.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150750	21			0	en-GB	Deleting field names				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154479	22			0	en-GB	To remove a field name from the query, click the column header of the field and choose the \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> command from the context menu for the column.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155764	277			0	en-GB	Save query				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148481	278			0	en-GB	Use the \<emph\>Save\</emph\> icon on the Standard Bar to save the query. You will see a dialogue box that asks you to enter a name for the query. If the database supports schemas, you can also enter a schema.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154362	279			0	en-GB	Schema				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754	280			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"DBACCESS_COMBOBOX_DLG_SAVE_AS_ET_SCHEMA\"\>Enter the name of the schema that is assigned to the query or table view.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156717	281			0	en-GB	Query name or table view name				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253	282			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"DBACCESS_EDIT_DLG_SAVE_AS_ET_TITLE\"\>Enter the name of the query or table view.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163805	23			0	en-GB	Filtering data				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154964	24			0	en-GB	To filter data for the query, set the desired preferences in the lower area of the Design View. The following lines are available:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146916	25			0	en-GB	Field				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156372	26			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FIELD\"\>Enter the name of the data field that you referred to in the Query. All settings made in the lower rows refer to this field.\</ahelp\> If you activate a cell with a mouse click you'll see an arrow button, which enables you to select a field. The "Table name.*" option selects all data fields and the criteria is valid for all table fields.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145150	148			0	en-GB	Alias				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146315	149			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_ALIAS\"\>Specifies an alias. This alias will be listed in a query instead of the field name. This makes it possible to use user-defined column labels.\</ahelp\> For example, if the data field has the name PtNo and, instead of that name, you would like to have PartNum appear in the query, enter PartNum as alias.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155959	193			0	en-GB	In an SQL statement, aliases are defined as following:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149922	194			0	en-GB	SELECT column AS alias FROM table.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159335	195			0	en-GB	For example:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148478	196			0	en-GB	SELECT "PtNo" AS "PartNum" FROM "Parts"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148485	27			0	en-GB	Table				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3163665	28			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_TABLE\"\>The corresponding database table of the selected data field is listed here.\</ahelp\> If you activate the a cell with a mouse click, an arrow will appear which enables you to select another table of the current query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154207	29			0	en-GB	Sort				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150979	30			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_ORDER\"\>If you click the cell, you can select from the sorting options: ascending, descending and not sorted.\</ahelp\> Text fields will be sorted alphabetically and numeric fields numerically. For most databases, administrators can set the sorting options.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150384	31			0	en-GB	Visible				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146133	32			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_VISIBLE\"\>If you mark the \<emph\>Visible\</emph\> property for a data field, that field will be visible in the query\</ahelp\>. If you only use a data field to formulate a condition, you do not necessarily need to show it.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154714	33			0	en-GB	Criteria				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145134	34			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_CRIT\"\>Specifies the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"criteria \"\>criteria \</link\>by which the content of the data field should be filtered.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152477	35			0	en-GB	or				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154585	36			0	en-GB	Here you can enter one additional criterion for filtering in each line. Multiple criteria in one column will be connected by an OR link.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148800	150			0	en-GB	You can also use the context menu of the line headers in the lower area of the query design to insert another line for functions:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148419	151			0	en-GB	Functions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153233	152			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select a function to run in the query here.\</ahelp\> The functions you can run here depend on the database.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id8760818				0	en-GB	If you are working with the HSQL database, the list box in the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> row offers you the following options:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150307	179			0	en-GB	Option				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166430	180			0	en-GB	SQL				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152993	181			0	en-GB	Effect				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155377	162			0	en-GB	No function				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155533	163			0	en-GB	No function will be executed.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166420	164			0	en-GB	Average				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145268	183			0	en-GB	AVG				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486	165			0	en-GB	Calculates the arithmetic mean of a field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149979	166			0	en-GB	Count				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154260	184			0	en-GB	COUNT				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155810	167			0	en-GB	Determines the number of records in the table. Empty fields can either be counted (a) or not (b).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151333	197			0	en-GB	a) COUNT(*): Passing an asterisk as the argument counts all records in the table.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152889	198			0	en-GB	b) COUNT(column): Passing a field name as an argument counts only fields in which the field name in question contains a value. Null values (empty fields) will not be counted.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153067	168			0	en-GB	Maximum				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148840	185			0	en-GB	MAX				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159221	169			0	en-GB	Determines the highest value of a field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146866	170			0	en-GB	Minimum				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148604	186			0	en-GB	MIN				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157982	171			0	en-GB	Determines the lowest value of a field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154828	172			0	en-GB	Sum				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147070	187			0	en-GB	SUM				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154536	173			0	en-GB	Calculates the sum of values of associated fields.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148820	174			0	en-GB	Group				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145375	188			0	en-GB	GROUP BY				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149438	175			0	en-GB	Groups query data according to the field name selected. Functions are executed according to the specified groups. In SQL, this option corresponds to the GROUP BY clause. If a criterion is added, this entry appears in the SQL HAVING.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156038	189			0	en-GB	You can also enter function calls directly into the SQL statement. The syntax is:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156340	190			0	en-GB	SELECT FUNCTION(column) FROM table.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155075	191			0	en-GB	For example, the function call in SQL for calculating a sum is:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154591	192			0	en-GB	SELECT SUM("Price") FROM "Article".				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159205	176			0	en-GB	Except for the \<emph\>Group\</emph\> function, the above functions are so-called Aggregate functions. These are functions that calculate data to create summaries from the results. Additional functions that are not listed in the list box might be also possible. These depend on the specific database system in use and on the current state of the Base driver.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148651	177			0	en-GB	To use other functions not listed in the list box, you must enter them under \<emph\>Field\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155098	178			0	en-GB	You can also assign aliases to function calls. If the query is not to be displayed in the column header, enter the desired name under \<emph\>Alias\</emph\>.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155539	199			0	en-GB	The corresponding function in an SQL statement is:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149425	200			0	en-GB	SELECT FUNCTION() AS alias FROM table				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144431	201			0	en-GB	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154614	202			0	en-GB	SELECT COUNT(*) AS count FROM "Item"				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154610	203			0	en-GB	If you run this function, you cannot insert any additional columns for the query other than receiving these columns as a "Group" function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154644	204			0	en-GB	\<emph\>Examples\</emph\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151120	205			0	en-GB	In the following example, a query is run through two tables: an "Item" table with the "Item_No" field and a "Suppliers" table with the "Supplier_Name" field. In addition, both tables have a common field name "Supplier_No."				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155144	206			0	en-GB	The following steps are required to create a query containing all suppliers who deliver more than three items.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153240	207			0	en-GB	Insert the "Item" and "Suppliers" tables into the query design.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148807	208			0	en-GB	Link the "Supplier_No" fields of the two tables if there is not already a relation of this type.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3161652	209			0	en-GB	Double-click the "Item_No" field from the "Item" table. Display the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> line using the context menu and select the Count function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151009	210			0	en-GB	Enter >3 as a criterion and disable the Visible field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145601	211			0	en-GB	Double-click the "Supplier_Name" field in the "Suppliers" table and choose the Group function.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147512	212			0	en-GB	Run the query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148638	213			0	en-GB	If the "price" (for the individual price of an article) and "Supplier_No" (for the supplier of the article) fields exist in the "Item" table, you can obtain the average price of the item that a supplier provides with the following query:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153045	214			0	en-GB	Insert the "Item" table into the query design.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149802	215			0	en-GB	Double-click the "Price" and "Supplier_No" fields.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153554	216			0	en-GB	Enable the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> line and select the Average function from the "Price" field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155597	217			0	en-GB	You can also enter "Average" in the line for the alias name (without quotation marks).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151191	218			0	en-GB	Choose Group for the "Supplier_No" field.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155547	219			0	en-GB	Run the query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147549	247			0	en-GB	The following context menu commands and symbols are available:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154172	248			0	en-GB	Functions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150414	249			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides a row for selection of functions.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149872	153			0	en-GB	Table Name				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147246	154			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides the row for the table name.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145117	155			0	en-GB	Alias Name				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155754	156			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides the row for the alias name.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153298	157			0	en-GB	Distinct Values				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147500	158			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Applies only distinct values to the query.\</ahelp\> This applies to records containing data that appears several times in the selected fields. If the \<emph\>Distinct Values\</emph\> command is active, you will see only one record in the query (DISTINCT). Otherwise, you will see all records corresponding to the query criteria (ALL).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150436	159			0	en-GB	For example, if the name "Smith" occurs several times in your address database, you can choose the \<emph\>Distinct Values\</emph\> command to specify in the query that the name "Smith" will occur only once.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152352	160			0	en-GB	For a query involving several fields, the combination of values from all fields must be unique so that the result can be formed from a specific record. For example, you have "Smith in Chicago" once in your address book and "Smith in London" twice. With the \<emph\>Distinct Values\</emph\> command, the query will use the two fields "surname" and "city" and return the query result "Smith in Chicago" once and "Smith in London" once.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149825	161			0	en-GB	In SQL, this command corresponds to the DISTINCT predicate.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148926	37			0	en-GB	Formulating filter conditions				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153162	38			0	en-GB	When formulating filter conditions, various operators and commands are available to you. Apart from the relational operators, there are SQL-specific commands that query the content of database fields. If you use these commands in the $[officename] syntax, $[officename] automatically converts these into the corresponding SQL syntax. You can also enter the SQL command directly. The following tables give an overview of the operators and commands:				20130618 17:22:18
