helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153674	109			0	en-GB	Querying text fields				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149134	110			0	en-GB	To query the content of a text field, you must put the expression between single quotes. The distinction between upper-case and lower-case letters depends on the database in use. LIKE, by definition, is case-sensitive (though some databases are not so strict).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149302	111			0	en-GB	Querying date fields				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150510	112			0	en-GB	Even if you want to filter by a date, you must place the expression between single quotation marks. The following formats are valid: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS and YYYY/MM/DD HH:MM:SS as well as YYYY.MM.DD HH:MM:SS				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150427	228			0	en-GB	Querying Yes/No fields				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149523	229			0	en-GB	To query Yes/No fields, use the following syntax for dBASE tables:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153180	230			0	en-GB	Status				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147481	231			0	en-GB	Query criterion				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155187	232			0	en-GB	Example				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156092	233			0	en-GB	Yes				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152414	234			0	en-GB	for dBASE tables: not equal to any given value				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151265	236			0	en-GB	=1 returns all records where the Yes/No field has the status "Yes" or "On" (selected in black),				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152450	238			0	en-GB	No				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150997	239			0	en-GB	.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155331	240			0	en-GB	=0 returns all records for which the Yes/No field has the status "No" or "Off" (no selection).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154179	241			0	en-GB	Null				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147035	242			0	en-GB	IS NULL				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159385	243			0	en-GB	IS NULL returns all records for which the Yes/No field has neither of the states Yes or No (selected in grey).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157888	244			0	en-GB	The syntax depends on the database system used. You should also note that Yes/No fields can be defined differently (only 2 states instead of 3).				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145772	113			0	en-GB	Parameter queries				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157312	114			0	en-GB	You must place the variable between square brackets (=[x]) to create a query with variable parameters. Alternatively, you can use an equal sign followed by a colon (=:x). When the query is executed, the program will display a dialogue box asking you for the expression to which the variable x should be assigned.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150818	273			0	en-GB	If you query several parameters at the same time, you will see a list field in the dialogue box containing all of the parameters and an input line alongside each one. Enter the values, preferably from top to bottom, and press the Enter key after each line.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157855	115			0	en-GB	Parameter queries with placeholders (*, _) or special characters (for example, ?) are not possible.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157537	116			0	en-GB	If you formulate a parameter query and you save it with the variables, you can later create a query in which only the variables have to be replaced by the expressions that you want. $[officename] asks for these variables in a dialogue box as soon as you open the query.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151035	139			0	en-GB	Parameter Input				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153596	140			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>The \<emph\>Parameter Input\</emph\> dialogue box asks you which variables you defined in the query. Enter a value for each query variable and confirm by clicking \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150585	138			0	en-GB	Parameter queries are also used for \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170203.xhp\" name=\"sub-forms\"\>sub-forms\</link\>, since they work exclusively with queries for which the values to be invoked are read internally from a variable.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153645	274			0	en-GB	A parameter query can have the following form in an SQL statement:				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156731	275			0	en-GB	select * from 'addresses' where 'name' = :placeholder				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145181	135			0	en-GB	SQL Mode				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147013	220			0	en-GB	SQL stands for "Structured Query Language" and describes instructions for updating and administering relational databases.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152570	136			0	en-GB	In $[officename] you do not need any knowledge of SQL for most queries, since you do not have to enter the SQL code. If you create a query in the query design, $[officename] automatically converts your instructions into the corresponding SQL syntax. If, with the help of the \<emph\>Switch Design View On/Off\</emph\> button, you change to the SQL view, you can see the SQL commands for a query that has been created previously.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152412	226			0	en-GB	You can formulate your query directly in the SQL code. Note, however, that the special syntax is dependent upon the database system that you use.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146842	227			0	en-GB	If you enter the SQL code manually, you can create SQL-specific queries that are not supported by the graphical interface in \<emph\>Query design\</emph\>. These queries must be executed in native SQL mode.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149632	223			0	en-GB	By clicking the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14030000.xhp\" name=\"Run SQL command directly\"\>\<emph\>Run SQL command directly\</emph\>\</link\> icon in the SQL view, you can formulate a query that is not processed by $[officename].				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-GB	Join Properties				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154015				0	en-GB	\<bookmark_value\>links;relational databases (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inner joins (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>joins in databases (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>left joins (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>right joins (Base)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>full joins (Base)\</bookmark_value\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154015	1			0	en-GB	Join Properties				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3151205	2			0	en-GB	If you double-click a connection between two linked fields in the query design, or if you choose \<emph\>Insert - New Relation\</emph\>, the \<emph\>Join Properties\</emph\> dialogue box will appear. These properties will be used in all queries created in the future.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155066	17			0	en-GB	Tables involved				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153924	16			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_RIGHT_TABLE\"\>Specifies two different tables that you want to join.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155766	15			0	en-GB	Fields involved				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148994	14			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Specifies two data fields that will be joined by a relation.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159267	3			0	en-GB	Options				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147340	13			0	en-GB	Type				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3152482	4			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>Specifies the link type of the selected link.\</ahelp\> Some databases support only a subset of the possible types.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155334	5			0	en-GB	Inner Join				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155936	6			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>With the internal join, the results table contains only the records for which the content of the linked fields is the same.\</ahelp\> In $[officename] SQL this type of link is created by a corresponding WHERE clause.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156372	7			0	en-GB	Left Join				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3166450	8			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>With the left join, the results table contains all fields of the left table and only those fields of the right table for which the content of the linked fields is the same.\</ahelp\> In $[officename] SQL this type of link corresponds to the LEFT OUTER JOIN command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155607	9			0	en-GB	Right Join				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150647	10			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>With the right join, the results table contains all fields of the right table and only those fields of the left table for which the content of the linked fields is the same.\</ahelp\> In $[officename] SQL this type of link corresponds to the RIGHT OUTER JOIN command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158215	11			0	en-GB	Full Join				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id3163665	12			0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DLG_QRY_JOINTYPE\"\>For a full join, the results table contains all fields of the left and right tables.\</ahelp\> In the SQL of $[officename] this type of link corresponds to the FULL OUTER JOIN command.				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	hd_id0305200912031976				0	en-GB	Natural				20130618 17:22:18
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010101.xhp	0	help	par_id0305200912031977				0	en-GB	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Inserts the keyword NATURAL into the SQL statement that defines the relation. The relation joins all columns that have the same column name in both tables. The resulting joined table contains only one column for each pair of equally named columns.\</ahelp\>				20130618 17:22:18
